ig02-rolling-update-maxsurge-maxunavailable\"\n178\t  - \"ch06-fig03-recreate-vs-rolling\"\n179\t  - \"ch06-fig04-workload-resource-decision-tree\"\n180\t  - \"ch06-fig05-statefulset-vs-deployment-identity\"\n181\t  - \"ch06-zenith-control-loop-instantiated\"\n182\t---\n183\t\n184\t# Chapter 6 Outline — Fleets, Not Vessels\n185\t\n186\t## Chapter-type note (read first)\n187\t\n188\t`chapter_type: content`. No contract exemptions apply. Drafting must deliver every required element:\n189\t\n190\t| Required element | Strictness | Where it lands |\n191\t|---|---|---|\n192\t| `# Chapter 6: Fleets, Not Vessels` | required | top |\n193\t| `## *\"Nobody sails one Pod\"*` | required | line 2 |\n194\t| Metadata line (weight / complexity / novelty) | required | after subtitle |\n195\t| `## Attention Budget` | required | before epigraph |\n196\t| Epigraph blockquote | expected | after Attention Budget |\n197\t| `## 🧭 Soundings` | expected | after epigraph — **8 questions** (content baseline) |\n198\t| `## Why This Chapter Matters` | **required** | after Soundings |\n199\t| `## What You'll Learn` | expected | after Why-This-Chapter-Matters |\n200\t| `## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1–#3` | **required, min 2** | after §3, §5, §8 |\n201\t| `★ Fixed Point` ×6 | **required, min 1** | §1, §3, §4, §5, §6, §7 |\n202\t| `**Dead Reckoning:**` ×1 min | **required** | §4 — rolling-update mechanics stated flat, no fleet register |\n203\t| `⚠ Navigational Hazards` ×2 | expected, min 1 | §5 (the revision rule), §7 (the three sibling-resource traps, one root cause) |\n204\t| `☀️ Zenith` | expected | §9 |\n205\t| `## Exam Alert` | **required** | after §9 |\n206\t| `## Practice Questions` | **required** | 19 questions |\n207\t| `## Chapter Summary` | **required** | table |\n208\t| `## The Voyage Ahead` | **required** | closing — name locked 2026-04-19 |\n209\t| `🏆 Safe Harbor` | expected | chapter close |\n210\t\n211\tHeading form follows the shipped chapters exactly: `## ⚪ §1 — The Resource That Holds the Intent`, and checkpoints as `## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1`.\n212\t\n213\t**Zenith:** exactly one, per Part 18.10. `ch06-zenith-control-loop-instantiated` in §9. This chapter carries five concept diagrams plus the Zenith — the same load as Chapters 4 and 5, at the upper end of the 2–8 band. `ch06-fig04` (the decision tree) will be the tempting one to inflate into a second Zenith because it is the figure readers will photograph. Resist. Its job is reference; §9's job is recognition.\n214\t\n215\t**Attention Budget guidance for drafting.** Nine sections, five distinct costs:\n216\t\n217\t| Section | Cost | Why |\n218\t|---|---|---|\n219\t| §1 | medium | One reframe (you will almost never create a Pod) plus a three-layer ownership chain that has to survive six later chapters |\n220\t| §2 | low | The reader already owns the control loop from Ch 3. This section makes it concrete and adds one field |\n221\t| §3 | medium | The selector mechanism, plus a consequence (template labels must agree) that is not obvious |\n222\t| §4 | **high** | Two strategies, two tunable bounds with defaults, and a safety property that depends on Chapter 5's probes. The chapter's densest block |\n223\t| §5 | medium | Small closed set of `kubectl rollout` verbs, plus one exact rule about what does and does not create a revision |\n224\t| §6 | medium | One distinction, drawn precisely, against a documented misconception — and a deliberate forward reference the reader must be told about |\n225\t| §7 | low | Three resources, each with one defining property |\n226\t| §8 | medium-high | An abstraction jump: resources that did not exist until someone declared them, and controllers you write |\n227\t| §9 | low | Synthesis |\n228\t\n229\t*\"If you only have 15 minutes\"* should point at **§1 plus the decision tree at the close of §7**, then Bearings #3. That is the highest exam-points-per-minute route through the chapter: the ownership chain is the structural key, and the decision tree is where traps #21, #22 and #23 all resolve.\n230\t\n231\t**Recommended split point** for the two-session reader: after Bearings #2 (end of §5). §1–§5 are the Deployment arc, start to finish. §6–§9 are the rest of the family and what the pattern turns into.\n232\t\n233\t---\n234\t\n235\t## Arc-outline inheritance\n236\t\n237\tAuthoritative input: `.pipeline-state/book-outline/arc-outline.md` § \"Chapter 6 — Fleets, Not Vessels\". Carried forward without modification:\n238\t\n239\t- **Covers**: **D1.1** — ReplicaSet; Deployment; rolling update; `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`; Recreate; revisions; rollout history; rollback; pause/resume; StatefulSet (introduced with siblings); DaemonSet; Job; CronJob; `kubectl scale`; the HPA concept; custom resources and the operator pattern (definition).\n240\t- **Prerequisites**: Ch 4 (objects, selectors), Ch 5 (Pod, probes, requests).\n241\t- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]** — from Ch 3–5. Named anchors: the control loop (Ch 3) now visible in a named controller; selectors (Ch 4) as the ReplicaSet→Pod join; probes (Ch 5) as what makes a rolling update safe.\n242\t- **Question budget**: 8 Soundings · 10 Bearings · 19 Practice · 37 total. Bearings raised to 15 below.\n243\t- **Figures**: six anchors, listed verbatim in `figures_planned`.\n244\t- **Depth band**: standard-plus.\n245\t- **Blocking gaps**: G8 (update mechanics, rollout, rollback) and G10 (CRDs, operator pattern). **Status: both are CLOSED.** Two *new* blocking gaps have opened that B1 did not anticipate — see § Open questions #2.\n246\t\n247\t### Debts falling due in this chapter\n248\t\n249\tFive published chapters defer material here by name. This is the highest inbound-debt chapter in Part II. Draft knowing the reader was told to expect each one."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2c44320-c026-4b9a-b495-33fa90758087","uuid":"8f60481f-f855-4f55-8fcc-f865f6c67166","timestamp":"2026-08-24T18:41:45.177Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lod