{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NtqzdPkLuqBc8aHsig1yc2","type":"tool_result","content":"892:[Omitted long matching line]\n981:Here is the clause that makes it click: once a custom resource is installed, users create and access its objects using `kubectl`, **just as they do for built-in resources like Pods** [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23]. Nothing about the tooling changes. `kubectl get`, `kubectl describe`, `kubectl apply`, labels, selectors, namespaces, RBAC, all of it works on the new kind, because the new kind lives in the same API.\n1086:[Omitted long matching line]\n1346:A) RBAC permissions granting access to the new resource\n1390:[Omitted long matching line]\n1440:Chapter 7 introduces the scheduler: how a Pod gets placed, what it is actually weighing, and the four mechanisms (`nodeSelector`, affinity, taints, and tolerations) you use to influence a decision you do not make yourself. It also settles a question this chapter raised and walked away from: a DaemonSet is supposed to run on every node, so what happens when a node has been marked as one that workloads should stay off?"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2c44320-c026-4b9a-b495-33fa90758087","uuid":"e64ca9ce-a242-408d-b388-0f2a65dbbfad","timestamp":"2026-08-24T18:45:12.755Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"892:[Omitted long matching line]\n981:Here is the clause that makes it click: once a custom resource is installed, users create and access its objects using `kubectl`, **just as they do for built-in resources like Pods** [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23]. Nothing about the tooling changes. `kubectl get`, `kubectl describe`, `kubectl apply`, labels, selectors, namespaces, RBAC, all of it works on the new kind, because the new kind lives in the same API.\n1086:[Omitted long matching line]\n1346:A) RBAC permissions granting access to the new resource\n1390:[Omitted long matching line]\n1440:Chapter 7 introduces the scheduler: how a Pod gets placed, what it is actually weighing, and the four mechanisms (`nodeSelector`, affinity, taints, and tolerations) you use to influence a decision you do not make yourself. It also settles a question this chapter raised and walked away from: a DaemonSet is supposed to run on every node, so what happens when a node has been marked as one that workloads should stay off?","numLines":6,"totalLines":6}}
