{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeN57zdp6KBXHjsCPApmm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have what I need. Verified against the shipped chapters, the five prior Stage-14 ledgers, B3, the curriculum snapshot, and all five ch-06 diagnostics. Four things I found change what this stage can claim — flagged inline below.\n\n---\n\n# Knowledge-Base Manifest — KCNA Chapter 6\n\n**Book:** Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate · **Chapter:** 6 — Fleets, Not Vessels\n**Stage:** 14 — Knowledge Base Update · **Date:** 2026-08-24\n\n**Inputs consulted:** the finalized chapter (`chapter-06-fleets-not-vessels.md`, on disk, 145,618 bytes); `ch-06/integration.md` (Stage 13, 284 lines); `ch-06/outline.md` frontmatter (`kb_tags` — 55 concepts, 10 commands); all five `ch-06/diagnostics/` reports; `ch-01`, `ch-03`, `ch-04`, `ch-05` and **`ch-07`** `kb-manifest.md`; `book-outline/{retrieval-architecture,chapter-lineup,domain-analysis,arc-outline}.md`; `sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md`; the seven published chapters.\n\n## ⚑ Gate status — one anomaly, two closures, three carried\n\n**⚑ This manifest runs out of order.** Chapter 7's Stage 14 completed before Chapter 6's, because ch-06 was withdrawn (`2bb971b`) and re-run. Chapter 7's ledger therefore recorded a **six-item \"Chapter 6 debt\"** and asked for it to be settled before the re-run. The re-run has happened; I discharge the debt below and correct one item that Stage 13 got backwards.\n\n**✅ Closed at Chapter 6, first: the strongest fact-accuracy outcome in the book.** Stage 6 inspected **236 claims — 214 tag instances, 100% resolving to a supplied snapshot and matching it; 0 contradicted; 5 FAIL findings covering 11 instances; 10 WARN.** All five FAILs and five of ten WARNs were then actually remediated in the finalized chapter. I verified each one line by line:\n\n| Finding | Stage 6 verdict | Finalized chapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| **F-1** — `D1.1` and the domain name asserted as CNCF taxonomy, untagged | FAIL | **Fixed** — `D1.1` removed from the metadata line; ID now appears only in YAML frontmatter |\n| **F-2** — six exam-format / exam-likelihood claims, incl. Exam Alert \"descending order of likelihood\" | FAIL, \"the load-bearing one\" | **Fixed, all six** — Exam Alert now reads \"descending order of how much of this chapter depends on them\"; the other five phrases are absent from the shipped file |\n| **F-3** — \"nearly the only place in Kubernetes where the API says *no*\" | FAIL, corpus supplies three counterexamples | **Fixed** — superlative dropped, pedagogical point kept verbatim as Stage 6 drafted it |\n| **F-4** — \"The most common workload resource is the Deployment\" | FAIL, frequency claim | **Fixed** — rewritten to the sourced *recommendation* form, tag added |\n| **F-5** — \"you will see the word in `kubectl get pods` output\" | FAIL | **Fixed** — softened to \"a word you will meet in tooling output\" |\n| **W-1** — Pod-template nesting rule sourced to the DaemonSet page | WARN | **Fixed** — attributed visibly, plus an AUTHOR-REVIEW recording the research gap |\n| **W-2** — \"through both\" attributes set-based support to `matchLabels` | WARN | **Fixed** — rewritten exactly as recommended |\n| **W-3** — dropped \"by default\" on StatefulSet ordinals | WARN, \"the hedge is load-bearing\" | **Fixed** — one word restored |\n| **W-4** — §1 Fixed Point simplification that §4 contradicts in spirit | WARN | **Fixed** — hedged with the forward pointer Stage 6 proposed |\n| **W-5** — `qq81z`, an impossible generated-name suffix | WARN | **Fixed** — now `qq8jz` |\n| **W-6** — fig04/fig05 anchor–caption transposition | WARN, structural | **Open** — recorded in AUTHOR-REVIEW, correctly, because fixing it requires a matching `image-specs.md` edit |\n\nAlso remediated from the other diagnostics: curriculum-alignment's single **absent** sub-objective (**D1.1-16 orphaning** — now covered, four occurrences in §3), and question-quality's two redundant Practice slots (Q3 and Q7 re-cut to close the `revisionHistoryLimit` and zero-pair gaps). **This is the second chapter where blocking findings were raised and discharged, and the first where every FAIL was.**\n\n**✅ Closed at Chapter 6, second: research plumbing is healthy for the second chapter running.** 21 snapshots supplied, 20 cited, every citation resolving to a real file. No recovery script needed. The failure that broke Chapter 5 has not recurred.\n\nCarried forward, unresolved:\n\n- **Chapter 2 still has never run Stages 13–14.** Six chapters on. Chapter 6 retrieves from Ch 3, 4 and 5 but not Ch 2, so it adds no new pressure — but §8's \"fourth socket\" pays off a theme Chapter 2 *named*, and there is no ledger row holding that theme's origin.\n- **Chapter 4 still has no fact-accuracy audit.** Chapter 5's ledger identified the root cause (Stage 6's declared input is `draft-v2.md`, which does not exist at that point in the graph). Chapters 5, 6 and 7 all audited cleanly against `draft-v1.md`, which is evidence the fix is a one-line input-resolution change.\n- **The KB write path is still broken. Sixth manifest to say so.** `=== WRITE` appears exactly twice in all of `certcomp`: in the prompt that asks for it, and in a stray Proxmox-era file under `_llm_write_archive/`. `certcomp/tools/` does not exist, so the materializers Chapters 4 and 5 supplied were never saved. `Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/` is still absent from disk. Replay order is now **ch-01 → ch-03 → ch-04 → ch-05 → ch-06 → ch-07**.\n\n## ⚑ The Chapter 7 debt — discharged, with one correction that reverses a Stage 13 fix\n\n| # | What Chapter 7 assumed | Verdict against the shipped Chapter 6 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ch 6 **§1** covers Deployments/ReplicaSets and the create-the-missing-Pod behaviour | ✅ **DISCHARGED.** §1 is the ownership chain; §2 is the create-the-missing-Pod demonstration. Ch 7's three pointers all say §1, which is where the reader enters the material. Sound. |\n| 2 | Ch 6 **§7** covers DaemonSets and one-per-node distribution | ✅ **DISCHARGED.** §7, verified at ch-06:880–894. |\n| 3 | Ch 6's **Voyage Ahead ends on the scheduler gap** | ✅ **DISCHARGED.** \"Between 'this Pod object exists' and 'this container is executing on a machine' sits a decision nobody in this chapter made… You know how a fleet is described. Next you find out how a berth is assigned.\" |\n| 4 | Ch 6 **§7 plants the DaemonSet-tolerations tease** — \"you'd already met the mechanism in disguise\" | ✅ **DISCHARGED — and Stage 13 got this one wrong. See below.** |\n| 5 | Ch 6 exists as the third of \"Chapters 4 through 6\" | ✅ Trivially discharged. |\n| 6 | Ch 7 §7's \"same shape as every controller in Chapter 6\" | ✅ **Now a stronger claim than when Ch 7's ledger flagged it.** Ch 6 §9's Zenith *is* the six-controllers-one-loop figure. Ch 7's ledger recommended re-pointing it at Ch 3 §6; that is still the better anchor for the loop's *definition*, but the Ch 6 attribution is no longer a reference to a withdrawn sentence. Downgrade from blocking to optional. |\n\n### ⚑ Do not apply Stage 13's Fix #11. Chapter 7:696 is accurate as written.\n\nStage 13 reports that \"Chapter 6 says neither of those things\" and that \"the phrases *nothing else will* and *in disguise* appear nowhere in chapter-06,\" and recommends rewriting Chapter 7's line 696 into a hand-back.\n\n**Both phrases are in the shipped chapter, in one sentence, at ch-06:894:**\n\n> One more thing to bank, because Chapter 7 collects it. DaemonSets keep running on nodes where **nothing else will** — nodes the cluster has fenced off from ordinary workloads entirely. And you have already met the mechanism that makes this possible, **in disguise**: it has been holding those networking and logging agents in place on every node since you first saw them running everywhere in Chapter 3's census. Chapter 7 unmasks it *[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 §4 — taints, tolerations, and the fence DaemonSets step over]*.\n\nChapter 7's callback is a faithful paraphrase, and the re-drafted Chapter 6 discharged debt item 4 exactly as Chapter 7's ledger asked. **Applying Fix #11 would replace a correct callback with a weaker one and lose the \"in disguise\" setup that Chapter 7 pays off.** Likely cause of the miss: Stage 13's own §7 grep matched at line 894 and reported the line as omitted, so the phrase never entered its comparison — the compound-emoji/long-line grep failure mode CLAUDE.md warns about.\n\nNote also that this sentence carries the **correct** `Ch 7 §4` pointer. Stage 13's Fix #1 targets a *different* pointer, at ch-06:965, which closes §7 and does say `§5`. That fix is right: Ch 7 §4 is *When the Berth Refuses You* (taints); §5 is *Placing Pods Relative to Each Other*. **Chapter 6 currently points at Ch 7 §4 in one place and Ch 7 §5 in another for the same material.**\n\n---\n\n## Glossary entries added to `Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/glossary.md`\n\n**63 terms contributed — 51 defined · 3 partial · 9 reserved · 6 status corrections to existing rows.**\n\nStage 13's coverage table counted 8 terms needing entries; my count is far higher because Stage 13 was measuring *undefined* terms while this ledger records every term the chapter *defines*, which is the bulk of Chapter 6. Appended as a Chapter 6 section rather than merged into the A–Z, following the Ch 04/05/07 precedent and for the same reason: this file is append-only under the current pipeline, and re-transcribing prior chapters' prose to preserve one alphabet is exactly the drift Rule 5 forbids.\n\n| Term | Definition (from chapter) | First appearance |\n|---|---|---|\n| **workload resource** ★ | \"you use workload resources that manage a set of Pods on your behalf, and these resources configure controllers that make sure the right number of the right kind of Pod are running, to match the state you specified\" | Chapter 6 §1 — **closes Chapter 5's reservation** |\n| **Deployment** | \"manages a set of Pods to run an application workload, usually one that doesn't maintain state,\" and \"provides declarative updates for Pods *and ReplicaSets*\" | Chapter 6 §1 |\n| **ReplicaSet** | \"to maintain a stable set of replica Pods running at any given time… often used to guarantee the availability of a specified number of identical Pods\" | Chapter 6 §1 |\n| **Pod template** | \"specifying the data of new Pods it should create\"; \"exactly the same schema as a Pod, except that it is nested and carries no `apiVersion` or `kind` of its own\" | Chapter 6 §1 — **closes Chapter 5's `pod-template` tag** |\n| **owner reference** | `metadata.ownerReferences` \"specifies what resource the current object is owned by\"; \"it is through this link that the ReplicaSet knows the state of the Pods it maintains\" | Chapter 6 §3 |\n| **cascading deletion** | \"Kubernetes checks for and deletes objects that no longer have owner references… background cascading deletion is the default\" | Chapter 6 §3 |\n| **orphan** | \"Dependents that survive their owner\"; \"by default Kubernetes deletes dependents rather than leaving them behind\" | Chapter 6 §3 |\n| **`maxUnavailable`** | \"the maximum number of Pods that can be unavailable during the update… **The absolute number is calculated from a percentage by rounding down. The default value is 25%.**\" | Chapter 6 §4 |\n| **`maxSurge`** | \"the maximum number of Pods that can be created *over* the desired number of Pods… **The absolute number is calculated from a percentage by rounding up. The default value is 25%.**\" | Chapter 6 §4 |\n| **`Recreate`** | \"all existing Pods are killed before new ones are created\" | Chapter 6 §4 |\n| **revision** | \"created if and only if the Deployment's Pod template (`.spec.template`) is changed. Other updates, such as scaling the Deployment, do not create a revision\" | Chapter 6 §5 |\n| **`revisionHistoryLimit`** | \"the number of old ReplicaSets to retain to allow rollback,\" default 10; set to zero and \"a new rollout **cannot be undone**\" | Chapter 6 §5 |\n| **StatefulSet** | \"runs a group of Pods and maintains a sticky identity for each of them\"; Pods \"created from the same spec but are **not interchangeable**\" | Chapter 6 §6 — **closes Chapter 1's and Chapter 5's reservation** |\n| **DaemonSet** | \"ensures that all (or some) nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to the cluster, Pods are added to them\" | Chapter 6 §7 |\n| **Job** | \"creates one or more Pods and continues to retry execution until a specified number of them successfully terminate\" | Chapter 6 §7 — **closes Chapter 3's reservation** |\n| **CronJob** | \"creates Jobs on a repeating schedule\"; \"one CronJob object is like one line of a crontab file on a Unix system\" | Chapter 6 §7 |\n| **custom resource** | \"an extension of the Kubernetes API that is not necessarily available in a default Kubernetes installation… **On their own, custom resources let you store and retrieve structured data.**\" | Chapter 6 §8 |\n| **operator pattern** | \"combines custom resources and custom controllers\" | Chapter 6 §8 |\n| *(45 further defined rows — full text in the append block)* | | |\n\n### Rule 6 — canon conflicts, recorded not resolved\n\nI checked Chapter 6 against every shard it could plausibly collide with — `control-loop.md`, `absent-component-pattern.md`, `spec.md`, `status.md`, `label-selector.md`, `kubernetes-object.md`, `pod.md`, `pod-lifetime.md`, `pod-phase.md`, `restart-policy.md`, `probe.md`, `resource-request.md`, `namespace.md`, `cluster-scoped-resource.md`, `nodeselector.md`, `taint.md`, `toleration.md`. **No factual contradiction with any of them.** Two naming conflicts and one shipped numeric error, all recorded and none resolved here.\n\n#### ⚑ CONFLICT 1 — the absent-component pattern has two competing canonical names, and Chapter 6 published the other one\n\nThis is the finding this stage exists to catch, and it is the reverse of what it looks like at first glance.\n\n`absent-component-pattern.md` (Chapter 3's shard) carries a header in capitals: **\"USE THIS NAME. DO NOT RE-COIN IT.\"** Its canonical name is *\"absent-component pattern\"*; its canonical short form is *\"an object without its component does nothing.\"*\n\n`retrieval-architecture.md` (**B3, the ratified retrieval design**) names the same theme differently, at line 15: *\"**the object exists but nothing happens without the component**\" — Ingress without a controller, NetworkPolicy on an unsupporting CNI, `kubectl top` without metrics-server, VPA not shipped by default. Naming it once and retrieving it by name turns four gotchas into one rule.*\n\n**Chapter 6 §8 retrieves B3's string, near-verbatim, and instructs the reader to retain it by name:**\n\n> **Name this shape, because you are going to meet it again.** *The object exists but nothing happens without the component.*\n\nand again in the Bearings #3 answer key: *\"**Name the pattern, because you will retrieve it by name.**\"*\n\nSo Chapter 6 is not re-coining — it is using B3's phrase. **The Chapter 3 shard is the artifact that invented a competing canonical string**, and it did so while forbidding exactly that. Two authorities, two \"retrieve it by name\" instructions, two different strings, and one of them is now shipped in reader-facing prose twice.\n\n**Do not resolve this by overwriting either.** Both are legitimate and the choice has consequences: B3's phrase is descriptive and reads naturally in prose; the shard's *\"absent-component pattern\"* is a shorter handle and the only one of the two that works as a noun in a Chapter 17 synthesis. **The author's call, and it is now urgent** — Chapters 10, 13 and 17 each owe a by-name retrieval, and Chapter 6 has already set a precedent in print.\n\n**Related: the instance roster is now five members across three different four-item lists.**\n\n| Instance | Ch 3 chapter text | Ch 3 shard | B3 | **Ch 6 §8** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ingress without an ingress controller | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| `kubectl top` without metrics-server | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| VPA not shipped by default | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| NetworkPolicy on a non-enforcing CNI | ✗ (the shard's flagged missing bearing) | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |\n| **CRD with no controller** | — | — | — | ✓ **new** |\n\nChapter 3 promised the reader \"four more times.\" Chapter 6 supplies a **fifth** instance and drops NetworkPolicy from its own list of four. The count claim in Chapter 3 is still satisfiable, but only if someone decides which four. Chapter 6's instance is arguably the best of the five — it is the only one where the reader *creates* the orphaned object themselves.\n\n#### ⚑ CONFLICT 2 — the competency count is 13, Chapter 5 shipped \"twelve,\" and Chapter 6 propagated it\n\nChapter 1's ledger flagged this and I re-verified it from the cached authority. `sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md` enumerates:\n\n```\n44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization   (4)\n28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage                 (4)\n16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging                      (2)\n12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Ecosystem and Principles; Community and Collaboration  (3)\n```\n\n4 + 4 + 2 + 3 = **13**. Chapter 1's domain table is right; B2 (`chapter-lineup.md:7, :11`) says 12 twice and is wrong.\n\n**The escalation:** Chapter 1's ledger asked for B2 to be corrected \"before Chapter 19's synthesis.\" It wasn't, and the error has since left the outline and entered print. `chapter-05:448` ships, in reader-facing prose:\n\n> …falls under **Troubleshooting**, one of the twelve named KCNA competencies **[source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23]**\n\nThat is a factual error about a certifying body, source-tagged to the very document that disproves it. It arrived as a *remediation* — Chapter 5's Stage 6 finding C2 replaced an unsupportable claim with this one, and inherited B2's bad number in the process.\n\nChapter 6 repeats \"twelve named competencies\" in its metadata AUTHOR-REVIEW comment, and ch-06's own curriculum-alignment audit states it as fact. **Chapter 6's instance is an HTML comment and will not ship** — materially lower severity than Chapter 5's — but it is a third propagation, and it means the audit instrument and the ledger now disagree.\n\n**Fix, in order: correct B2 (one token, twice); correct `chapter-05:448` (one word, shipped text); strike the count from ch-06's AUTHOR-REVIEW.** Outside this stage's write scope.\n\n#### ✅ Obligations discharged, all clean\n\n1. **`control-loop.md`'s Chapter 6 row is DISCHARGED, and it is the theme's first *planned* landing.** The shard records the obligation as: *\"Instantiate the loop in ReplicaSet — 'a control loop you can watch working in real time.' B2 calls this the second beat of the book's spine.\"* Chapter 6 §2 opens **\"Chapter 3 promised you a control loop you could watch working in real time. This is it,\"** which is Chapter 3's own forward-bearing text (`chapter-03:950`) quoted back — the verbatim-handoff discipline `spec.md` calls the model for every deferred-term handoff. And §9 extends it into the Zenith. **Chapters 4, 5 and 7 all retrieved this theme unplanned; Chapter 6 is the rung B3 designed.**\n2. **✅ `control-loop.md`'s *common* shape is now cleanly instanced, which repairs a blur Chapter 5 created.** Chapter 5's ledger recorded a near-miss: the kubelet is Chapter 3's *uncommon* direct-control case, presented there as if canonical. Chapter 6 §2 states the common case explicitly — *\"the controller does not create that Pod itself… The ReplicaSet controller writes a wish to the API server and something else does the work\"* — with the source tag. Together with Chapter 7 §7 (the scheduler), the book now has two clean instances of the common shape against Chapter 5's one uncommon one. **The blur is repaired without anyone editing Chapter 5.**\n3. **✅ `status.md`'s Chapter 6 obligation is DISCHARGED.** The shard records Chapter 4's stated reuse plan — *\"Chapter 6 [reads it] against a replica count.\"* Practice Q2 does exactly that and its key uses the documentation's own three-replica Deployment example, which is the example `spec.md` records Chapter 4 as having borrowed *for its shape only* and deferred here. **Second of that shard's three reuses to land; only Chapter 13's remains.**\n4. **✅ `label-selector.md`'s Chapter 6 obligation is DISCHARGED** — *\"A ReplicaSet knows which Pods are *its* Pods by selector.\"* §3 delivers it, and correctly identifies itself as the first of the shard's four uses to be collected. No drift: the four resources supporting the structured form match the shard exactly, and the post-W-2 `matchLabels` wording is consistent with the shard's \"exact equivalence\" claim.\n5. **✅ `pod.md` / `pod-lifetime.md`'s Chapter 6 obligation is DISCHARGED, and it is the book's cleanest seam.** `pod.md` records: *\"Answer 'if Pods are designed to be replaced, who does the replacing?' — Chapter 5's Voyage Ahead hands this over as Chapter 6's opening question.\"* Chapter 6's Why This Chapter Matters opens on that question **quoted verbatim from `chapter-05:1460`**, §1 opens on it again in four words, and Soundings Q8 grades the reader's memory of the handoff before either. Three-layer discharge of one commitment.\n6. **✅ `pod-lifetime.md` gains a refinement, not a contradiction.** The shard's rule is \"replaced by a new, near-identical Pod **with a different UID**.\" Chapter 6 adds \"a different **name** and a different UID\" — correct for generated-name workloads, and Practice Q5's key handles the exception explicitly: *\"D is the trap worth understanding, because it is true of a different resource\"* (StatefulSet Pods keep their ordinal-derived names). **Recommend the shard gain that qualifier at reconcile.** Chapter 6 got the harder version right.\n7. **✅ `probe.md`'s readiness half acquires a second job.** Chapter 5 taught readiness as traffic removal. Chapter 6 §4 turns it into release safety — *\"a version that never reports ready never replaces the version that works\"* — using Chapter 5's exact source sentence. `chapter-05:858` forward-bears to Ch 6 §4 for precisely this. Both halves of that seam are correct.\n\n### ⚑ Status changes to existing rows (apply at reconcile — cannot be done by append)\n\n| Row | Current text | Correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tier 3 · **workload resource** (ch-05) | \"Named here only because Pod disposability forces it to exist… Chapter 6 owns the treatment\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §1**, verbatim from the workloads overview |\n| Tier 3 · **`pod-template`** (ch-05) | \"deferred to Ch 6 — correct; the tag is aspirational\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §1.** ⚑ The nesting rule is sourced to the **DaemonSet** page; the Deployment/ReplicaSet equivalent is not cached |\n| Tier 3 · **StatefulSet** (ch-01, ch-05) | reserved → Ch 6, \"⚑ NO BEARING\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §6.** The missing Ch 5 bearing is moot |\n| Tier 3 · **Job** (ch-03) | reserved → Ch 6, \"name-dropped only\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §7** |\n| Tier 3 · **EndpointSlice** (ch-03) | \"Ch 9 · Ch 3 §2 (controller list) · name-dropped only\" | ⚑ **DEPTH ESCALATION, still reserved.** Ch 6 uses it **twice inside a graded answer key, with a source tag**, and never defines it. See below |\n| Tier 3 · **VPA** (ch-03) | reserved → Ch 17 | **CONFIRMED.** Ch 6 §8 surfaces the *behaviour* (\"a vertical autoscaler that isn't shipped by default\") without naming VPA. Correct handling |\n\n### ⚑ Three \"priority\" gaps Stage 13 raised are already-reserved terms with recorded owners\n\nStage 13 ran with `[no matching knowledge-base shards found]` and therefore could not see the ledger. Its three priority glossary gaps re-rank as follows:\n\n| Stage 13 finding | Actual ledger state | What Chapter 6 actually needs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **EndpointSlice** — \"needs entry, priority\" | Reserved → **Ch 9** since Chapter 3 | Not a glossary gap. But it is the one term in Chapter 6 whose *depth* outruns its reservation: a reserved term should not carry a source tag inside a graded answer key. Either add `*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — EndpointSlice]*` or cut the sentence to \"the Service's endpoint objects\" |\n| **RBAC** — \"needs entry, priority\" | Reserved → **Ch 12 §2** since Chapter 4 (`label-selector.md`'s ⚑ THE EXCEPTION) | Not a glossary gap. A cross-bearing at ch-06:981 would help; Q18's option A is a distractor and needs none |\n| **`nodeSelector` / node affinity** — \"needs entry, priority\" | **Already defined.** `nodeselector.md` and `node-affinity.md` exist as Chapter 7 shards | Not a gap at all. Chapter 6's Voyage Ahead names all four mechanisms and hands to Chapter 7 explicitly. The only loose use is §7's DaemonSet paragraph, where a cross-bearing is optional |\n\n**Genuinely new reservations from Chapter 6:** `volume claim template` (→ Ch 11, **no bearing** — the one Chapter 6 term deferred without a pointer), and `API-server aggregation` (→ characterized but undefined; **no owner chapter assigned by B2**). Everything else Chapter 6 defers carries a pointer.\n\n---\n\n## Concept shards added at `Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/{slug}.md`\n\n**Fifteen created.** Every slug is drawn from `outline.md`'s `kb_tags.concepts` so context-packer lookups round-trip, following the Chapter 4/5/7 precedent.\n\n- `concepts/workload-resource.md` — **created** (§1 definition, the reframe, and the §7 decision tree) — **closes Chapter 5's reservation**\n- `concepts/ownership-chain.md` — **created** (§1, fig01) — *six later chapters depend on this*\n- `concepts/deployment.md` — **created** (§1, §4, §5)\n- `concepts/replicaset.md` — **created** (§1, §2, §3; HPA folded in as a note)\n- `concepts/selector-template-agreement.md` — **created** (§3; membership-as-query, the API rejection, adoption, overlapping selectors)\n- `concepts/owner-reference.md` — **created** (§3; ownership vs selection, cascading deletion, orphans)\n- `concepts/rolling-update.md` — **created** (§4; both bounds, the asymmetric rounding, what makes it safe)\n- `concepts/recreate-strategy.md` — **created** (§4; the contrast and the legitimate-choice argument)\n- `concepts/revision.md` — **created** (§5; the iff rule, rollback, `revisionHistoryLimit`, pause/resume)\n- `concepts/statefulset.md` — **created** (§6)\n- `concepts/daemonset.md` — **created** (§7)\n- `concepts/job.md` — **created** (§7)\n- `concepts/cronjob.md` — **created** (§7)\n- `concepts/custom-resource.md` — **created** (§8; CRD, dynamic registration, the honest limitation)\n- `concepts/operator-pattern.md` — **created** (§8)\n\n**Deliberate structural choices, with reasons.**\n\n`selector-template-agreement.md` and `owner-reference.md` are **split along the boundary the chapter itself draws** — *\"Ownership is exclusive; selection is not.\"* The documentation is explicit that ownership \"is different from the labels and selectors mechanism,\" Bearings #1 Q5 grades exactly that distinction, and one shard holding both would let the fact that matters fall between its halves. Adoption goes with the selector shard because it is a selection consequence; cascading deletion and orphans go with ownership.\n\n`job.md` and `cronjob.md` are **split, and the split is the pedagogy.** §7's hazard block names the conflation as one of its three traps and defuses it by pointing at the shape: *\"The CronJob is not a kind of Job. It is a thing that makes Jobs, the way a Deployment is not a kind of ReplicaSet but a thing that makes ReplicaSets.\"* Modelling Deployment/ReplicaSet as two shards and Job/CronJob as one would break the parallel a reader is being taught to notice.\n\n`rolling-update.md` and `recreate-strategy.md` are **split** because the discrimination between them is graded twice — Practice Q9 and Bearings #2 Q3 — and the near-miss is subtle: `maxSurge: 0` gets you a zero-headroom rollout but *not* the no-overlap guarantee, so it is not a substitute for `Recreate`. Q9's option C is materially true and still the wrong reason. A single `deployment-strategy.md` would bury that.\n\n**Not created, with reasons.** `horizontalpodautoscaler` — one paragraph in §2, correctly at its ceiling; Chapter 17 owns autoscaling and §2 bears to it. Folded into `replicaset.md` as a scaling note. `pod-interchangeability` — the thesis of `statefulset.md`, not a separate object. `stable-pod-identity` — same shard; splitting would separate the identity from the storage that follows it, which is the whole point of fig05. `deployment-strategy`, `maxsurge`, `maxunavailable`, `minreadyseconds`, `readiness-gated-rollout`, `stuck-rollout` — one mechanism viewed from six sides, held together in `rolling-update.md`. `rollout`, `rollout-history`, `rollback`, `revision-history-limit`, `pause-rollout`, `resume-rollout` — likewise in `revision.md`, because the chapter's own rule (*a revision **is** a ReplicaSet*) is what makes all six comprehensible at once. `customresourcedefinition`, `custom-controller`, `declarative-api`, `dynamic-registration` — in `custom-resource.md`; the CRD-alone-does-nothing fact only survives if the noun and the verb are described in the same file. `horizontal-scaling` / `vertical-scaling` / `manual-horizontal-scaling` — one paragraph, folded. `replicationcontroller-legacy` — three sentences whose whole content is \"recognize it, know it's superseded\"; a shard would be longer than the material.\n\n**No `commands/` shards.** All ten `kubectl` verbs in `kb_tags` appear as usage rather than as taught command surface, following the Chapter 4/5 precedent of leaving the command surface to Chapter 8. `kubectl rollout`'s six-subcommand table is inside `revision.md` because the verbs are unintelligible without the revision rule.\n\n**No shard *appends*, deliberately.** Six shards have their Chapter 6 obligations discharged and would ideally gain a section. I am not emitting `=== APPEND` blocks for `concepts/*.md`, because none of those files exists on disk and the recovery script opens append targets with mode `a` — appending to a non-existent path would create a shard containing *only* the append, silently discarding the head that Chapters 3–5 composed. The discharges are recorded above and belong in a reconcile pass, not in a blind append.\n\n---\n\n## Voice-exemplar candidates nominated\n\n**Not written to `voice-exemplars.md`** — Rule 1. Nominations only, for author ratification.\n\n| Function | Excerpt | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **☀️ Zenith** | \"Six resources. One shape. **You have been looking at the same diagram for the entire chapter.**… Nothing was added to the loop to accommodate any of them. Nothing needed to be.\" | **Strongest in the chapter and a new kind of Zenith for the series.** Every prior Zenith delivered a synthesis the reader hadn't seen; this one delivers the *recognition that they already had it*. Nominate as the canonical recognition-Zenith exemplar. |\n| **Extended Analogy** | \"A Deployment's Pods are a watch rotation… A StatefulSet's Pods are a pilot who knows this harbor. The order is not 'one pilot.' It is *this* pilot, who has the approach notes for this specific entrance… **Confusing them costs you in exactly one direction:** treat interchangeable crew as irreplaceable and you carry unnecessary machinery; treat irreplaceable crew as interchangeable and you lose data.\" | **Strong, and the book's best §18.15 Extended Analogy so far.** Every noun maps, the analogy earns its length, and the closing clause converts a metaphor into an asymmetric-risk rule. |\n| **why-wrong explanation** | *(Bearings #1 A3, option A)* \"**A is wrong, but it is the *right* intuition.** The runaway is exactly what would happen if the object were accepted, which is why the API refuses it. If you picked A, you understood the mechanism and did not know about the validation. **That is a good place to be missing a point from.**\" | **Strong, and a genuinely new move.** It separates *reasoning quality* from *answer correctness* and credits the first while marking the second. Pairs with Chapter 5's self-concealing-miss exemplar as the two poles of the why-wrong register. |\n| **★ Fixed Point** | \"The discriminator between the two bounds is the rounding, and it runs in opposite directions: **surge rounds up, unavailable rounds down.** Both defaults therefore err toward keeping more capacity online, **which is the sentence to memorize if you can only keep one — you can rebuild both rules from it.**\" | **Strong.** A *compressing* Fixed Point: it hands the reader one sentence that regenerates two rules plus their rationale. Structurally different from Chapter 4's and Chapter 5's, and worth having both patterns in canon. |\n| **Logbook Entry** | \"Overlapping selectors fail in two directions, and neither direction announces itself as a configuration mistake, which is what makes them expensive… **The prevention is a single habit, and it costs nothing:** give every workload one label that is genuinely unique to it, and never hand-write a selector by copying somebody else's.\" | **Strong.** Models §18.15 exactly — skippable without losing the thread, and it adds a habit the body prose cannot. Notable for being a Logbook Entry with **no war story**: it is a mechanism narrative, which sidesteps the fabricated-anecdote risk entirely. |\n| **⚓ Worth Securing** | \"Self-healing and scaling are not two features. They are one loop, seeing the same kind of gap, taking the same action. If you can hold that, you have most of what this chapter teaches.\" | **Strong.** Collapses two chapter-length topics into one sentence and tells the reader its weight without inflating it. |\n| **🪝 Snag** | \"'We installed the CRD, created an object of the new kind, and nothing happened.' That is the correct behavior. There is no controller. **You gave the cluster a new noun and no verb.**\" | **Strong.** Nine words carrying the §8 Fixed Point. The best compression in the chapter. |\n| **chapter-opening / curiosity gap** | \"Here is the answer, and it is stranger than 'a thing called a Deployment.' **Kubernetes has no special replacement machinery at all.** It has the same control loop you met in Chapter 3, handed a count to hold and a template to copy from… This chapter has fewer ideas in it than its table of contents suggests.\" | **Strong.** Opens by *deflating* its own subject, which is an unusual and very on-brand move — the confidence is in the reduction. |\n| **opening epigraph (original)** | \"A standing order outlives the watch that received it. That is the whole reason you write it down.\" | **Strong.** Original Lodestar quote that states the chapter's thesis before the chapter argues it, and the phrase \"standing order\" then does load-bearing work in §1, §9 and the Safe Harbor. |\n| **closing epigraph (original)** | \"You can write the standing order in an afternoon. Finding the water to carry it out is the other half of the work.\" | **Strong.** Rhymes with the opening epigraph *and* hands off to Chapter 7's subject. Nominate the pair together; they only do this work as bookends. |\n| **generation-effect prompt** | \"the labels in the Pod template do not match the selector… Work out what happens next before you read the answer. **It takes about ten seconds and the answer is worth deriving yourself.**\" | **Moderate–strong.** Textbook Part 10 generation effect, and the second clause is what makes it work — it prices the effort honestly instead of exhorting. |\n| **🏆 Safe Harbor** | \"You arrived able to read what a Pod was telling you. You leave able to state what should be true about a group of them and hand the maintenance of that statement to something that never sleeps and never forgets. **That is not a larger vocabulary. It is a different job.**\" | **Strong.** Identity-transformation framing (skill Part 3) stated as a fact about the reader rather than a claim about the book. |\n\n**Deliberately not nominated:** every line carrying an unsourced claim about reader populations — *\"the most consequential wrong answer in this chapter,\" \"the most common wrong answer,\" \"you are in the majority,\" \"you are in good company,\" \"the one most people arrive with,\" \"the first thing most people check,\" \"the figure readers will photograph.\"* Same reasoning Chapters 3, 4, 5 and 7 applied. Guardrail #8's ruling is still open and promoting this register would ratify it into brand canon before the author has decided. (Three of these claims are separately *licensed* — see the guardrail section — but licensed is not the same as exemplary.)\n\n### ⚑ The small-hours beat is now three chapters running\n\nChapter 5's ledger flagged this and asked for diversification \"at chapter five of twenty, [rather] than at chapter twelve.\" The count since:\n\n| Chapter | Beats |\n|---|---|\n| Ch 4 | `status.md`'s 🪝 Snag — \"usually at two in the morning\" |\n| Ch 5 | opening — \"a terminal at two in the morning with a pager still buzzing\"; §7 — \"at three in the morning\" |\n| **Ch 6** | Soundings Q1 — \"One of them dies at 3 a.m.\"; §8 — \"Every one of those is somebody's 2 a.m. runbook, turned into a loop that never sleeps\" |\n\nFive beats, three consecutive chapters. Chapter 6's §8 line is the best of the five and I have nominated its section for other reasons — but the register has crossed from signature into tic, and the request Chapter 5 made was not acted on. Two of Chapter 6's own §7 lines show the alternative works: \"Monday at 3 a.m.\" and \"02:00\" are schedule facts carrying no register at all. **This is now a house-style decision rather than a per-chapter note.**\n\n---\n\n## Objective coverage log\n\n**⚑ D1.1 CLOSES AT CHAPTER 6.** B2 assigns Kubernetes Core Concepts four consecutive chapters (3, 4, 5, 6) and Chapter 6 is the last of them. This is the first competency in the book to be opened and closed across multiple chapters, and the registry row changes accordingly.\n\n| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| D1.1 | Chapter 3 *(cluster layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n| D1.1 | Chapter 4 *(object layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n| D1.1 | Chapter 5 *(workload layer — the Pod)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n| **D1.1** | **Chapter 6** *(controller layer — closes the competency)* | **deep** | **2026-08-24** |\n\n**Coverage is near-complete and the scope discipline is the strongest in Part II.** Curriculum-alignment audited **36 sub-objectives: 35 covered, 1 absent, 0 partial** — and the one absent (**D1.1-16, orphaning**) was remediated in revision, so the shipped chapter is **36 / 36**. Every one of the eleven out-of-scope boundaries the outline set is held: `concurrencyPolicy`, `parallelism`, `backoffLimit`, Indexed Jobs, job-history limits, TTL, proportional scaling, `--cascade=orphan`, finalizers, OLM/Kubebuilder, and Knative/Argo-by-name all return zero hits. Blue/green, canary and A/B appear exactly once, inside the single authorized forward bearing.\n\n### ✅ The domain-allocation alarm is fully resolved\n\nChapter 5's ledger raised this as blocking before Chapter 6 drafted, projecting **16 points** for Chapter 6 alone. Chapter 7's ledger revised it to eleven. Chapter 6 claimed **~6%**, and with Chapter 7's number the domain now closes cleanly:\n\n| Chapter | Claimed | Objective |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch 2 — Containerization | ~9% | D1.4 |\n| Ch 3 — Cluster architecture | ~6% | D1.1 |\n| Ch 4 — Objects | ~6% | D1.1 |\n| Ch 5 — Pods | 7% | D1.1 |\n| **Ch 6 — Controllers** | **~6%** | **D1.1 (closes)** |\n| Ch 7 — Scheduling | 5% | D1.3 |\n| **Claimed** | **39% of 44%** | |\n| **Residual for D1.2 / Ch 8 (Administration)** | **5%** | |\n\n**D1.1 totals 25% across four chapters.** Five points remain for Chapter 8, which is tight but defensible and — unlike the 16-point projection — is a number Chapter 8 can be drafted *to* rather than around. **Recording it here because Chapter 8 is the next chapter to draft and this is the input it needs.**\n\n**The disclosure decision is still open, and Chapter 6 is now the sixth different phrasing.** Chapter 7's ledger recommended conforming to Chapter 7's dedicated italic line while keeping Chapters 2 and 5's inline source tags. Chapter 6 instead invented `Authored weight: ~6% of this book` — which, as Stage 13 notes, makes a *different claim* than every other chapter (\"of the exam\"). Stage 13's Fix #4 recommends adopting `chapter-05:190`'s form. **⚑ Note that Chapter 6's own AUTHOR-REVIEW instruction — \"match the exact disclosure phrasing used in the metadata lines of Chapters 2–5\" — is not executable**, because those four chapters use four different phrasings and Chapter 2 has no line in that pattern. There is no form to match; one has to be chosen. Chapter 7's ledger, Chapter 5's ledger and Stage 13 now all independently recommend the same resolution, and nobody has ruled.\n\n### ⚑ Ethical-guardrail status — Chapter 6, and one open item CLOSED\n\n| Chapter | Guardrail #3 (strawmanning) | Guardrail #8 (frequency claims) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch 1 | pass | pass |\n| Ch 2 | pass | pass — models the compliant phrasing |\n| Ch 3 | pass | **FAIL — open** (six unverifiable exam-frequency assertions) |\n| Ch 4 | pass | BORDERLINE (five practitioner-prevalence superlatives) |\n| Ch 5 | pass | BORDERLINE (four exam-frequency assertions) |\n| Ch 7 | ⚑ **FAIL — open** (competitor-pedagogy claim, L1113) | BORDERLINE (four superlatives) |\n| **Ch 6** | **pass** | **BORDERLINE — improved. Four unbacked claims, down from ten** |\n\n**✅ Chapter 6 is the first chapter since Chapter 2 to ship zero exam-frequency claims,** and it got there by remediation rather than by never having them: Stage 6's F-2 caught six, and all six were fixed. The Exam Alert's replacement framing — *\"in descending order of how much of this chapter depends on them\"* — is a **dependency** claim about the book, not a frequency claim about the exam, and it is the most defensible construction any chapter has shipped for that heading. **Recommend it as the pattern `reconcile.py` sweeps the other chapters to.**\n\n**✅ Stage 13's open verification item #14 is CLOSED, favourably.** Stage 13 could not check whether B1 trap #21 is `[source]` or `[inferred]`. It is **`[source]`** (`domain-analysis.md:524`), and so are **#22** (DaemonSet-for-several-copies, :525) and **#23** (Job/CronJob confusion, :526). All three of §7's ⚠ Navigational Hazards traps are source-backed, so B2's constraint at `chapter-lineup:184` — inferred traps must be described as \"easy to confuse,\" never as frequency — **does not bind them.** Chapter 6's \"the one most people arrive with,\" \"the disk misconception,\" and the hazard block's root-cause framing are all licensed. No edit needed.\n\n**Four claims remain unbacked**, and they are the same practitioner-prevalence register Chapters 4, 5 and 7 carry: *\"the most consequential wrong answer in this chapter\"* and *\"the most common wrong answer\"* (the Deployment-owns-Pods-directly error), *\"you are in the majority\" / \"you are in good company\"* (scaling-creates-a-revision), and *\"the first thing most people check\"* (Q18 option A). None maps to a numbered B1 trap. **Guardrail #8 is now four chapters running after Chapter 3's open FAIL, and Chapter 7's ledger already said the overdue thing is the ruling, not the edit.** I concur and will not re-litigate it per chapter.\n\n**Guardrail #3: clean, and worth noting given Chapter 7's fresh FAIL.** Chapter 6 makes no claim about other study guides or other publishers. Its one adversarial move — *\"Treating `Recreate` as always-wrong is its own trap\"* — is directed at a practice, defended immediately with three concrete cases, and explicitly re-framed as correct engineering. That is the construction Chapter 7's L1113 needed.\n\n**Everything else on the Part 14 checklist passes, three items unusually well.** Zero statistics of any kind appear in the chapter. The **Order/Truth balance is handled better than in any chapter so far**, in three separate places: §1's *\"The simple version… The full picture is that the number is written twice\"*; §4's proper `Dead Reckoning` block; and §6's *\"A loop left open on purpose\"*, which tells the reader outright that the storage half is deferred **and that the deferral is deliberate rather than an omission** — precisely guardrail #5. And the **v5.7 subject-dignity guardrail is clean**: every wry beat lands on the practitioner (the adopted debug Pod killed in seconds, the 2 a.m. runbook, the untidy `kubectl get rs` output), none on anyone harmed by a failure, and §4's stalled-rollout Snag closes on *\"Users experience nothing\"* — the one place a consequence narrative could have drifted toward third parties, and it turns toward the mechanism's success instead.\n\n---\n\n## Retrieval-practice ledger\n\n**7 tagged in-budget items · graded pool 34 (15 Bearings + 19 Practice) · rate = 20.6%.** B3's rung for Chapter 6 is **20%. Cleared** — by the thinnest margin in the book (Ch 5: 21.1%, Ch 7: 21.9%). Two further tagged items sit in Soundings (Q7, Q8), excluded from the budget by B3 but doing the spacing work. Independently computed and matching the question-quality audit's figure exactly.\n\n**Chapter 6 draws from three predecessors** — Ch 3, 4 and 5. Chapter 5 also drew from three; Chapter 7 from four. The breadth plateaued here and resumed climbing after.\n\n| Tested topic | Original chapter | Retested in |\n|---|---|---|\n| the control loop — two states and an action | ch 3 §6 | **ch 6** — Soundings Q7 *(excluded from budget)* |\n| a Pod is replaced, not rescheduled; who does the replacing was left open | ch 5 §4 | **ch 6** — Soundings Q8 *(excluded from budget)* |\n| desired state, current state, and the action, with the field name | ch 3 §6 | **ch 6** — Bearings #1 Q4 |\n| two independent selectors over one Pod | ch 4 §5 | **ch 6** — Bearings #1 Q5 |\n| readiness probes; a Pod that never reports ready | ch 5 §7 | **ch 6** — Bearings #2 Q4 |\n| `spec` holds what you asked for, `status` what is running | ch 4 §2 | **ch 6** — Practice Q2 |\n| the replacement Pod gets a new UID; recorded identity goes stale | ch 5 §4 | **ch 6** — Practice Q5 |\n| the five Pod phases; `Succeeded` means all terminated in success | ch 5 §5 | **ch 6** — Practice Q16 |\n| a controller reads `.spec`, acts, writes `.status` | ch 3 §6 | **ch 6** — Practice Q19 |\n\n### Quality notes\n\n- **Practice Q5 is the strongest retrieval item in the chapter and one of the best in the book.** Its distractor D is *true of a different resource* — StatefulSet Pods do keep their ordinal-derived names across replacement — and the key says so outright: *\"Which resource is holding the Pods decides which answer is right.\"* It converts a retrieval item into a discrimination item by using material from later in the same chapter as the trap.\n- **Practice Q19 is the chapter's best structural item.** It asks what a third-party controller and a built-in controller have *in common* and where each *runs* — two axes, one answer — and it is the direct precursor to Chapter 15's central recognition. Bearings #3 Q5 sets it up; Q19 grades it.\n- **✅ Chapter 7's improvement was inherited, not lost.** Chapter 5's ledger recorded that retrieval tags appeared only on stems, never in answer keys, and Chapter 7 fixed it. Chapter 6 does the same: Q2's key names Chapter 4's own forward-bearing (*\"Chapter 6 reads it against a replica count\"*), Q5's names Chapter 9's stake, Q16's names Chapter 5's phase table. A reader who misses an item is told where to go back to.\n- **⚑ Bearings #1 Q5 is tagged `ch4` but its load-bearing content is Chapter 6's own.** The stem is Chapter 4 §5 material; the answer's actual teaching — *\"Ownership is exclusive; selection is not\"* — is §3's. It works as written; it is doing less retrieval work than the tag implies. Same pattern the Chapter 5 ledger recorded for its own Bearings #1 Q5, which suggests a drafting habit rather than a one-off.\n- **⚑ One prescribed anchor dropped, at zero benefit.** The outline (line 420) specified that §3 should refer to `ch04-fig03-labels-selectors-join` **by name** rather than redraw the join — *\"which is also a spacing-effect retrieval at zero cost.\"* The figure exists at `chapter-04:806` and its caption is load-bearing (*\"sets, not a taxonomy\"*). Chapter 6 never mentions it. Recovering it costs one clause and buys a free retrieval event on the exact theme Chapter 6 was told to retrieve.\n\n### Cross-cutting themes — ⚑ §8 is the first landing site for two stalled themes\n\n| Theme | Introduced | Retrieved so far | Next |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **The control loop** (B3 headline) | Ch 3 §6 | Ch 4 · Ch 5 · ✅ **Ch 6 — FIRST PLANNED retrieval, and it lands as the Zenith** · Ch 7 | **Ch 11 (still unbeared)**, Ch 15, Ch 17 |\n| **Labels/selectors as the universal join** | Ch 4 §5 | Ch 5 · Ch 7 ×2 · ✅ **Ch 6 ×3 — the planned Ch 6 retrieval lands** | Ch 9, Ch 10 |\n| **The absent-component pattern** | Ch 3 §4, named | ✅ **Ch 6 §8 — FIRST retrieval in the book, four chapters late, and *by name*** ⚑ but see Conflict 1 | Ch 10 ×2, Ch 13, Ch 17 |\n| **\"Kubernetes defines an interface, the ecosystem implements it\"** | Ch 2 §4, named | ✅ **Ch 6 §8 — FIRST named recurrence in the book** | Ch 9 (CNI), Ch 11 (CSI), Ch 17 |\n| **Namespaced vs cluster-scoped** | Ch 4 §3 | Ch 5 §6 | ⚑ **Ch 6 walked past a free one** — Ch 12 §3, Ch 10, Ch 11 |\n\n**Chapter 6 §8 is the highest-value 1,200 words in the book so far, measured by theme architecture.** The Chapter 5 and Chapter 7 ledgers both recorded the absent-component pattern and the interface/ecosystem pattern as **\"still zero\"** — two of B3's nine themes, both named in print, neither ever retrieved, four and five chapters after their introduction. §8 lands both, in the same section, both explicitly by name:\n\n> **The fourth socket.** Chapter 2 showed you the move: Kubernetes defines an interface and lets the ecosystem implement it. You met it with the container runtime, with networking, and with storage, and you were told you would meet it once more at the API layer. **This is that.**\n\nThat is a named retrieval of Chapter 2's theme, delivered as a payoff rather than a reminder, and it is the only one in six chapters. **⚑ It is also the only retrieval in the book of a theme originating in Chapter 2 — the chapter with no ledger.** If Chapter 2 ever runs Stages 13–14, this is the row its theme table needs.\n\n**⚑ Both landings are compromised in the same way, and it is now a pattern with four instances.** §8 retrieves the absent-component pattern using B3's string rather than the shard's (Conflict 1), and §3 retrieves the universal join without `label-selector.md`'s canonical wording — *\"the label selector is the core grouping primitive in Kubernetes\"* appears nowhere in Chapter 6. Chapter 5 paraphrased the namespaced/cluster-scoped string; Chapter 7 paraphrased the universal-join string; Chapter 6 has now paraphrased one and published a rival coinage of another. **Four chapters have each invented their own phrasing of strings fixed precisely to prevent that.** Chapter 7's ledger set the deadline at \"before Chapter 10 drafts\" — three chapters remain, and the cost of deciding has now risen from a convention call to a shipped-text edit.\n\n**⚑ A free retrieval Chapter 6 walked past.** Practice Q18's option-B rebuttal reads *\"namespace has nothing to do with it. Custom resource objects live wherever their scope allows.\"* Custom resources are declared either namespaced or cluster-scoped, which makes this the most natural site the book will offer for retrieving Chapter 4 §3's boundary — the fact is *demonstrable* here rather than assertable, exactly the property Chapter 7's ledger identified for the Nodes case. One clause in the rebuttal. This theme still has **one** retrieval in six chapters, by paraphrase.\n\n**⚑ `reconciliation` remains undefined, and Chapter 6 held the line.** Chapter 5's ledger recorded the gap as worsening (the book grading readers on a word Chapter 3 promised to define); Chapter 7 did not use the word at all. **Chapter 6 uses it once, in §1's Fixed Point discussion — \"accepting it and reconciling toward it\" — and never in an answer key.** The gap has not closed but it has not grown for two chapters running, and both chapters demonstrate the mechanism can be described without the term. The one-appositive fix at Chapter 3's ★ Fixed Point remains the right close.\n\n### Forward commitments — two discharged, one overdue, seven new\n\n| # | Commitment | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Ch 13 must carry a Ch 8 retrieval item (version skew) | **OPEN** — untouched |\n| 2 | Ch 11 must retrieve the control loop | ⚑ **OPEN, now five chapters overdue.** Ch 6 bears to Ch 11 §4 (StatefulSet storage) and **does not carry the loop.** Ch 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7 have each passed it forward |\n| 4 | Ch 12 must **derive** the RBAC 2×2 from the namespaced boundary | **OPEN.** Ch 6 surfaces RBAC twice without a bearing |\n| 5 | Ch 9 must retrieve the Pod IP / shared namespace | **OPEN.** Ch 6 §2 bears to Ch 9 §1 — *\"this churn is exactly why something needs a stable name\"* — which is adjacent to the commitment but is about *names*, not the IP |\n| 6 | Ch 13's method must be \"read the phase before you read the logs\" | **OPEN** |\n| 7 | Ch 6 must open on \"if Pods are designed to be replaced, who does the replacing?\" | ✅ **DISCHARGED, three ways** — Why This Chapter Matters quotes `chapter-05:1460` verbatim, §1 re-opens on it in four words, and Soundings Q8 grades the handoff |\n| 8 | Ch 7, 13, 17, 18 must each retrieve requests/limits | Ch 7 discharged. Ch 13, 17, 18 **OPEN**; Ch 6 does not touch it, correctly |\n| 9–12 | Ch 7's four new commitments (`Pending` filter-vs-score, `Pending` capacity-vs-taint, Ch 17's scheduler socket, Ch 8's `unschedulable`) | **OPEN, unchanged** |\n| 13 | **Ch 15 must be the third sighting of the control loop, with desired state in a Git repository** | **NEW, and the largest.** §9 publishes it outright — *\"a controller whose desired state lives in a Git repository, and why that is the same technology rather than a new one\"* — and the Safe Harbor tells the reader *\"the third time is the one that matters.\"* B3 calls this the book's primary Zenith; Chapter 6 has now promised the reader it is coming, in print |\n| 14 | **Ch 15 §4 owns blue/green, canary and A/B** | **NEW.** §4's single authorized forward bearing is the only mention in the chapter, and it names the section |\n| 15 | **Ch 11 must complete the StatefulSet storage half** | **NEW.** §6's *\"A loop left open on purpose\"* tells the reader the deferral is deliberate and names what is owed: PV, PVC, StorageClass, access modes, the provisioning path. ⚑ Two facts were cut from §6 in revision that belong in `ch06-fig05`'s design brief, not the prose — see the AUTHOR-REVIEW at that anchor |\n| 16 | **Ch 9 must define the headless Service, as the thing that carries StatefulSet network identity** | **NEW.** §6 tells the reader \"you are responsible for creating it\" and defines nothing |\n| 17 | **Ch 13 §3 must diagnose a stuck rollout and identify which of the six causes fired** | **NEW.** §4 publishes the full list — readiness failure, image pull, quota, permissions, limit ranges, runtime misconfiguration — and explicitly assigns the diagnosis to Ch 13 |\n| 18 | **Ch 17 must resolve CRI, CNI, CSI and CRD into one story** | **NEW.** §8's fourth socket. ⚑ Chapter 6 pins `Ch 17 §6`; Chapter 2 published `Ch 17 §4` for the same synthesis. **Collision, and it is the one that matters most** — it is the section where four chapters' worth of theme converges |\n| 19 | **Ch 14 §6 must explain why Helm charts have a `crds/` directory** | **NEW.** Cheap to honour, and it is a `custom-resource.md` consequence rather than a Helm fact |\n| 20 | **Ch 10, 13 and 17 must retrieve the absent-component pattern using ONE string** | **NEW, and blocking on the author.** Chapter 6 has published a rival canonical phrasing. Whichever string wins, three chapters and possibly two shipped chapters need to match it |\n\n### ⚑ §N reservation collisions — the count is now nine, and Chapter 6 disagrees with itself once\n\nChapter 3's ledger proposed the convention (no `§N` in cross-bearings into undrafted chapters); Chapter 4 broke it fifteen times; Chapter 5 broke it seventeen. Chapter 6 adds nine conflicts. Stage 13 enumerated them and I confirm all nine, plus one Stage 13 identified as internal:\n\n| Destination | Claimants | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ch 13 §2** | Ch 2 / Ch 5 (*`ImagePullBackOff`*) · **Ch 6 §8** (*`kubectl top` without metrics-server*) | ⚑ conflict |\n| **Ch 13 §4** | Ch 5 (*`OOMKilled` / `Evicted`*) · **Ch 6 §2** (*metrics-server is what an HPA reads*) | ⚑ conflict |\n| **Ch 13 §3** | Ch 5 (*multi-container logs*) · **Ch 6 §4** (*diagnosing a stuck rollout*) | ⚑ conflict |\n| **Ch 6 internal** | §2 → `Ch 13 §4` · §8 → `Ch 13 §2` | ⚑ **Chapter 6 points at two different sections for metrics-server, in one chapter** |\n| **Ch 9 §1** | Ch 2 (*CNI*) · Ch 5 (*why a Service is necessary*) · **Ch 6 §2** (*why something needs a stable name*) | ⚑ now **three-deep**; Ch 2 has precedence |\n| **CNI's home in Ch 9** | Ch 2 → **§1** · **Ch 6 §7** → **§7** | ⚑ same subject, two sections |\n| **Ch 15 §4** | Ch 5 (*the delivery agent's identity*) · **Ch 6 §4** (*blue/green, canary, A/B*) | ⚑ conflict |\n| **Ch 17 extension-points synthesis** | Ch 2 → **§4** · **Ch 6 §8** → **§6** | ⚑ **the highest-stakes collision** — see commitment 18 |\n| **Ch 18 §3** | Ch 5 (*utilization vs requests*) · **Ch 6 §7** (*node-level log collection*) | ⚑ conflict |\n\n**✅ The house has already started fixing this, twice, independently.** Chapter 4's most recent re-draft (`73fd066`) stripped section numbers from **all three** of its Chapter 6 pointers, and Chapter 2's most recent commit is titled *\"Ch-02: drop §3 from the Ch 7 pointer.\"* That is the convention Chapter 3's ledger proposed, arriving by practice rather than by ratification. **Recommend ratifying it:** drop `§N` from forward bearings into undrafted chapters, keep it on backward bearings into published ones (where it is verifiable — and Chapter 6's are correct with the single `Ch 7 §5` exception), and let `reconcile.py` add numbers once destinations are final. Nine conflicts and one self-contradiction retire in one pass.\n\n### ⚑ The outline's numbering warning is now stale, and will mislead the next session\n\n`ch-06/outline.md` lines 18–30 and the chapter's own frontmatter both record that **§3 is pinned by `chapter-04` line 688** (*\"a controller's selector and the Pods it owns\"*), and that the two collisions from Chapter 1 and Chapter 2 therefore cannot be resolved by renumbering. `label-selector.md` records the same three-way claim.\n\n**That pin no longer exists.** Chapter 4's re-draft dropped the section number from that pointer and from both others. The recommended fixes do not change — StatefulSet genuinely lives at §6 and CRDs at §8, so `chapter-01:436` → `§6` and `chapter-02:600` → `§8` are still right — but the *constraint* recorded in the outline is gone, and a future session reasoning from it will conclude §3 is immovable when it isn't. **Correct the outline's § Open questions #1 and the chapter frontmatter at reconcile.**\n\n---\n\n## Operator notes\n\n1. **The write path is still broken. Sixth manifest.** `=== WRITE` appears twice in all of `certcomp`: in `pipeline/prompts/14_knowledge_base_update.md`, and in a stray Proxmox-era artifact at `_llm_write_archive/diagnostics/kb-manifest.md`. No parser. `certcomp/tools/` still does not exist, so the materializers Chapters 4 and 5 supplied were never saved. `Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/` is absent from disk. **Everything below is composed and none of it lands without one script run**, and replay order is load-bearing: **ch-01 → ch-03 → ch-04 → ch-05 → ch-06 → ch-07**. Chapters 1 and 3 emit *full* `glossary.md`, `objective-coverage.md` and `retrieval-log.md`; Chapters 4, 5, 6 and 7 emit *appends*. Running out of order silently discards the later chapters. Materializer below.\n2. **⚑ Do not apply Stage 13's Fix #11.** Chapter 7:696 is accurate. Full evidence above; this is the one recommendation in the integration report that would make the book worse.\n3. **Chapter 6's three blocking chapter-text fixes are all one token.** The `Ch 7 §5` → `§4` pointer at ch-06:965; the `## Exam Alert` → `## Exam Alert! 🚨` heading; and the internal metrics-server split. Stage 13's Fix #3 (topics on the three checkpoint headings) is a four-word edit and the skill template requires it.\n4. **Two ledger corrections outrank the chapter fixes**, because they are shipped errors in *other* files: `chapter-05:448`'s \"twelve named competencies\" (13, source-tagged to the document that says so) and B2's two instances of the same number. Neither is fixable from inside Chapter 6.\n5. **Chapter 8 is the next chapter to draft, and its number is now known: 5% of the exam, D1.2 Administration.** That figure is a residual, not a derivation — it is what remains of Kubernetes Fundamentals after six chapters. Worth putting in front of the author before Chapter 8's outline runs, because it is the first chapter in the book whose allocation is determined rather than chosen.\n6. **Chapter 2 remains the oldest open item.** Six chapters, no Stages 13–14, and Chapter 6 §8 just paid off a theme that originates there with no ledger row to record it.\n\n### Materializer — replay the KB blocks (ch-01 → ch-07)\n\n```python\n# save as certcomp/tools/replay_kb_blocks.py\nimport re, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nsys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\nBOOK = Path(r\"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\")\nMANIFESTS = [BOOK / \".pipeline-state\" / c / \"kb-manifest.md\"\n             for c in (\"ch-01\", \"ch-03\", \"ch-04\", \"ch-05\", \"ch-06\", \"ch-07\")]  # order is load-bearing\n\nWRITE  = re.compile(r\"^=== WRITE (?P<p>.+?) ===\\r?\\n(?P<b>.*?)^=== END WRITE ===\\r?$\",\n                    re.MULTILINE | re.DOTALL)\nAPPEND = re.compile(r\"^=== APPEND (?P<p>.+?) ===\\r?\\n(?P<b>.*?)^=== END APPEND ===\\r?$\",\n                    re.MULTILINE | re.DOTALL)\n\nfor man in MANIFESTS:\n    if not man.exists():\n        print(f\"SKIP (absent): {man}\"); continue\n    text = man.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    for rx, mode in ((WRITE, \"w\"), (APPEND, \"a\")):\n        for m in rx.finditer(text):\n            tgt = Path(m.group(\"p\").strip().replace(\"/\", \"\\\\\")).resolve()\n            tgt.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n            body = m.group(\"b\")\n            if not body.endswith(\"\\n\"):\n                body += \"\\n\"\n            with open(tgt, mode, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as fh:\n                fh.write(body)\n            print(f\"{'WROTE ' if mode=='w' else 'APPEND'} {tgt.relative_to(BOOK)}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/glossary.md ===\n\n---\n\n# Chapter 6 additions — Fleets, Not Vessels (2026-08-24)\n\n**Appended, not merged.** This file is append-only under the current pipeline. Book\nassembly merges alphabets mechanically.\n\nTerms contributed: **63** — 51 defined · 3 partial · 9 reserved · 6 status corrections.\n\n## ⚑ Status changes to EXISTING rows above (apply at reconcile — cannot be done by append)\n\n| Row | Current text | Correction | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tier 3 · **workload resource** | \"Named here only because Pod disposability forces it to exist… Chapter 6 owns the treatment\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §1** | The shipped chapter; verbatim from the workloads overview |\n| Tier 3 · **`pod-template`** | \"deferred to Ch 6 — correct; the tag is aspirational\" | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §1.** ⚑ The nesting rule is sourced to the DaemonSet page; the Deployment/ReplicaSet equivalent is not cached | Ch 6 §1 + its AUTHOR-REVIEW |\n| Tier 3 · **StatefulSet** | reserved → Ch 6, \"⚑ NO BEARING\" from Ch 5 | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §6.** Ch 5's missing bearing is moot | Ch 6 §6 |\n| Tier 3 · **Job** | reserved → Ch 6, \"name-dropped only\" at Ch 3 §2 | **CLOSED — defined at Ch 6 §7** | Ch 6 §7 |\n| Tier 3 · **EndpointSlice** | \"Ch 9 · Ch 3 §2 (controller list) · name-dropped only\" | ⚑ **DEPTH ESCALATION, still reserved.** Ch 6 uses it twice inside a graded answer key with a source tag, and never defines it. A reserved term should not carry a tag inside an answer explanation | Ch 6 Bearings #1 A5 |\n| Tier 3 · **VPA** | reserved → Ch 17 | **CONFIRMED.** Ch 6 §8 surfaces the behaviour without naming VPA. Correct handling | Ch 6 §8 |\n\n## ⚑ CROSS-CHAPTER CORRECTION — the competency count is 13, not 12\n\n`sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md` enumerates 4 + 4 + 2 + 3 = **13** named\ncompetencies. Chapter 1's domain table is right. B2 (`chapter-lineup.md:7, :11`) says 12\ntwice and is wrong. **`chapter-05:448` ships \"one of the twelve named KCNA competencies\"\nin reader-facing prose, source-tagged to the document that disproves it.** Chapter 6\nrepeats the number in an AUTHOR-REVIEW comment (not reader-facing) and ch-06's\ncurriculum-alignment audit inherits it. Three propagations from one outline error.\n\n**Fix order:** B2 (one token, twice) → `chapter-05:448` (one word, shipped) → strike the\ncount from ch-06's AUTHOR-REVIEW.\n\n---\n\n## Tier 1 — Defined at Chapter 6 (prose inherited verbatim)\n\n### A\n\n**adoption (controller)** — \"if a Pod has no owner reference, or an owner reference that is\nnot a controller, and it matches the selector, it will be immediately acquired.\" A\nReplicaSet \"is not limited to owning Pods produced by its own template,\" and if acquisition\nputs it over its desired count, \"the newly adopted Pod is immediately terminated.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3)\n> 🪝 Snag, verbatim: \"You create a debugging Pod with `app: web` on it, because that is what\n> the other Pods have. It is adopted by the ReplicaSet and killed within seconds. Nothing\n> errors. Nothing warns you.\"\n\n**available (Deployment sense)** — \"ready for at least `minReadySeconds`.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n> Distinct from *ready* (Ch 5 §7). Readiness is a probe result; availability is readiness\n> that has persisted. **The gap between the two is what makes a rollout safe rather than\n> merely gradual.**\n\n### C\n\n**cascading deletion** — \"Kubernetes checks for and deletes objects that no longer have\nowner references, like the Pods left behind when you delete a ReplicaSet… **background\ncascading deletion is the default**.\" [source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24]\n(Ch 6 §3)\n\n**CronJob** — \"creates Jobs on a repeating schedule,\" and \"one CronJob object is like one\nline of a crontab file on a Unix system.\" Meant for \"regular scheduled actions such as\nbackups and report generation.\" [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n> ★ Fixed Point wording: **\"CronJob: creates the same Job repeatedly, on a schedule.\"**\n> ⚠ It is not a kind of Job. It is a thing that *makes* Jobs — the same relationship as\n> Deployment → ReplicaSet, and Ch 6 teaches the two in parallel deliberately.\n\n**CronJob — idempotency requirement** — \"a CronJob creates a Job **approximately** once per\nexecution time of its schedule. The scheduling is approximate because there are\ncircumstances where two Jobs might be created, or none. Kubernetes tries to avoid those\nsituations but does not completely prevent them, therefore the Jobs you define **should be\nidempotent**.\" [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n> ⚑ Flagged with emphasis in the chapter and **never tested** — see the question-coverage\n> note in the chapter's AUTHOR-REVIEW.\n\n**CustomResourceDefinition (CRD)** — \"the API resource that lets you define custom\nresources. Defining a CRD object creates a new custom resource with a name and schema that\nyou specify, and the Kubernetes API then serves and handles the storage of it for you. This\nfrees you from writing your own API server, though the generic nature of the implementation\nmeans you have less flexibility than with API-server aggregation.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n\n**custom controller** — a client program following the controller contract: \"read and/or\nwrite to the Kubernetes API, following a control loop, reading an object's `.spec`, possibly\ndoing things, and then updating the object's `.status`.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> The chapter's gloss, worth keeping: \"**Nothing about that sentence is privileged. Anyone\n> can write a program that does it.**\"\n\n**custom resource** — \"an extension of the Kubernetes API that is not necessarily available\nin a default Kubernetes installation; it represents a customization of a particular\ninstallation.\" Once installed, \"users create and access its objects using `kubectl`, **just\nas they do for built-in resources like Pods**.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"A custom resource on its own stores and retrieves structured data. A\n> custom resource combined with a custom controller is the operator pattern.\"**\n> The limitation is verbatim and load-bearing: \"**On their own, custom resources let you\n> store and retrieve structured data.**\" That is all.\n\n### D\n\n**DaemonSet** — \"ensures that all (or some) nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to\nthe cluster, Pods are added to them; as nodes are removed, those Pods are garbage collected.\nDeleting the DaemonSet cleans up the Pods it created.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"One Pod per eligible node, added automatically as nodes join. The count\n> is a consequence of the cluster, not a setting.\"**\n> ⚑ **Hedged form is mandatory.** No cached sentence states that a DaemonSet has no\n> `replicas` field. The claim rests on \"creates a Pod for each eligible node\"\n> [k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] plus the HPA page naming DaemonSet as an object that\n> can't be scaled [k8s-docs-hpa-2026-08-24]. **Do not strengthen to field absence** until\n> the DaemonSet API reference is cached (research gap G-6A).\n\n**DaemonSet — selector immutability** — \"once a DaemonSet is created, its `.spec.selector`\ncan not be mutated,\" because \"mutating a pod selector can lead to the unintentional\norphaning of Pods.\" [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3, §7)\n> The only workload resource in the chapter that forbids the edit outright. Ch 6 uses it as\n> evidence for how hazardous selector mutation is in general.\n\n**DaemonSet — node-local facility** — \"a DaemonSet defines Pods that provide facilities\nlocal to nodes, and each Pod performs a job similar to a system daemon on a classic Unix or\nPOSIX server.\" Typical uses: a cluster storage daemon, a log-collection daemon, a\nnode-monitoring daemon, on every node.\n[source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n\n**declarative API** — \"you declare the desired state of your resource, and the controller\nkeeps the current state of Kubernetes objects in sync with your declared desired state.\nThis is in contrast to an imperative API, where you instruct a server what to do.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> Chapter 3's control loop, written by someone who is not the Kubernetes project.\n\n**Deployment** — \"manages a set of Pods to run an application workload, usually one that\ndoesn't maintain state,\" and \"provides declarative updates for Pods **and ReplicaSets**.\"\nYou create a Deployment; the Deployment creates a ReplicaSet; the ReplicaSet creates Pods\nin the background. [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §1)\n> ⚑ The second half of the definition is the part readers skim and the structural key to\n> §4 and §5. **Retain \"and ReplicaSets.\"**\n> Recommended over managing ReplicaSets directly: \"we recommend using Deployments instead\n> of directly using ReplicaSets\" [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n**Deployment — `.spec.replicas`** — \"an optional field specifying the number of desired\nPods, defaulting to 1.\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §1)\n> ⚑ The number is written **twice** — on the Deployment and on the ReplicaSet. The\n> Deployment is the author of the second copy; the ReplicaSet is where it is *acted on*.\n> The chapter states the simple version and then discloses the full picture, deliberately.\n\n**Deployment — `.spec.strategy`** — \"specifies the strategy used to replace old Pods with\nnew ones.\" `.spec.strategy.type` is `Recreate` or `RollingUpdate`, and \"**`RollingUpdate` is\nthe default**.\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §4)\n\n**dynamic registration** — custom resources \"can appear and disappear in a running cluster\nthrough dynamic registration, and cluster admins can update them independently of the\ncluster itself.\" [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> Practice Q18's distractor D depends on this: registration is not delayed, so \"waiting\n> longer will not help.\"\n\n### E\n\n**extension points (the six categories)** — API extensions (CRDs and the API aggregation\nlayer), controllers, scheduling extensions, API access extensions, kubectl plugins, and\ninfrastructure extensions. [source: k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> ⚑ This is the **fourth socket** of the interface/ecosystem theme Chapter 2 named — and\n> Ch 6 §8 is the theme's **first named recurrence in the book.** Ch 6 pins the synthesis at\n> `Ch 17 §6`; Chapter 2 published `Ch 17 §4`. Collision; see the retrieval ledger.\n\n### H\n\n**horizontal scaling** — \"the response to increased load is to deploy more Pods.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-hpa-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §2)\n\n**HorizontalPodAutoscaler** — \"an API resource plus a controller, running in the control\nplane, that periodically adjusts the desired scale of its target to match observed metrics\nsuch as average CPU utilization.\" Kubernetes implements it as \"a control loop that runs\n**intermittently rather than continuously**.\" Horizontal autoscaling \"does not apply to\nobjects that can't be scaled\" — the documentation's own example being a DaemonSet.\n[source: k8s-docs-hpa-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §2)\n> Deliberately one paragraph. Ch 13 owns metrics-server; Ch 17 owns the autoscaling\n> landscape. ⚑ Taught, summarized, cited in an answer key, and **never tested** — see the\n> chapter's question-coverage AUTHOR-REVIEW.\n\n### J\n\n**Job** — \"represent one-off tasks that run to completion and then stop. A Job creates one\nor more Pods and continues to retry execution until a specified number of them successfully\nterminate; as Pods complete, the Job tracks the successful completions, and when the\nspecified number is reached the Job is complete. Deleting a Job cleans up the Pods it\ncreated.\" [source: k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"Job: runs a task to completion, once.\"**\n> Still the control loop — the desired state is *completion* rather than a count of running\n> things. The Job \"will start a new Pod if the first one fails or is deleted, for example\n> due to node hardware failure or a node reboot.\"\n\n**Job — `restartPolicy` restriction** — a Job's Pod template \"may only use a `restartPolicy`\nof `Never` or `OnFailure`.\" [source: k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §7)\n> The chapter's gloss: \"once you have the concept, that restriction writes itself: **a Pod\n> that is always restarted can never complete.**\" Retrieves Ch 5's `restartPolicy` and\n> inverts the meaning of *exited*.\n\n### M\n\n**`maxSurge`** — \"the maximum number of Pods that can be created **over** the desired number\nof Pods. The value can be an absolute number or a percentage. **The absolute number is\ncalculated from a percentage by rounding up. The default value is 25%.**\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n\n**`maxUnavailable`** — \"the maximum number of Pods that can be unavailable during the update.\nThe value can be an absolute number or a percentage of desired Pods. **The absolute number\nis calculated from a percentage by rounding down. The default value is 25%.**\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n\n**`maxSurge` / `maxUnavailable` — the zero-pair constraint** — \"neither one can be zero if\nthe other is zero.\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n> The chapter's reason, worth keeping: \"a rollout that may neither exceed the desired count\n> nor drop below it has no legal move to make.\" Practice Q7 grades the *pair* semantics —\n> `maxSurge: 0` with `maxUnavailable: 1` is legal and is what you reach for on a\n> capacity-constrained cluster.\n\n**`maxSurge` / `maxUnavailable` — the asymmetric rounding** ⚠ — surge rounds **up**;\nunavailable rounds **down**. [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n> ★ Fixed Point, recorded as the canonical retrieval string and the chapter's single most\n> compressible fact: **\"Both defaults therefore err toward keeping more capacity online,\n> which is the sentence to memorize if you can only keep one — you can rebuild both rules\n> from it.\"**\n> 🪢 Mnemonic: **\"Surge is above the line. Unavailable is below it.\"**\n> ⚑ **The outline's worked example is WRONG and must not propagate to The Lodestar.** Ten\n> replicas at defaults gives a ceiling of **13** (⌈2.5⌉ = 3), not 12, and a floor of **8**\n> (⌊2.5⌋ = 2). Confirmed by Stage 6 against both the concept page and the API reference.\n\n**`minReadySeconds`** — \"the minimum number of seconds for which a newly created Pod should\nbe ready without any of its containers crashing for it to be considered available; it\ndefaults to 0, meaning a Pod counts as available the moment it is ready.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n\n### O\n\n**operator pattern** — \"combines custom resources and custom controllers.\" It \"aims to\ncapture the key aim of a human operator who is managing a service, someone with deep\nknowledge of how the system ought to behave, how to deploy it, and how to react if there\nare problems, and to write that knowledge as code that automates a task beyond what\nKubernetes itself provides.\" Operators \"are clients of the Kubernetes API that act as\ncontrollers for a custom resource,\" and the pattern \"lets you extend the cluster's behavior\nwithout modifying the code of Kubernetes itself.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23]\n(Ch 6 §8)\n\n**operator — where the controller runs** — \"the controller will normally run **outside of\nthe control plane**, much as you would run any containerized application: **for example, as\na Deployment**.\" [source: k8s-docs-operator-pattern-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> ⚑ This is the chapter's closing structural turn and **the direct precursor to Chapter\n> 15's central recognition.** The misconception it kills — that an operator is privileged,\n> or installed into the API server — makes Ch 15's delivery-tool-as-controller read as a\n> special case instead of the ordinary case. Bearings #3 Q5 and Practice Q19 both grade it.\n\n**orphan** — \"Dependents that survive their owner are called orphan objects, and by default\nKubernetes deletes dependents rather than leaving them behind.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3)\n> The route to producing them by accident is **mutating a selector**, so Pods a controller\n> used to claim stop matching it and nothing is left holding them.\n\n**owner reference** — `metadata.ownerReferences` \"specifies what resource the current object\nis owned by\"; all Pods acquired by a ReplicaSet carry the owning ReplicaSet's identifying\ninformation there, \"and it is through this link that the ReplicaSet knows the state of the\nPods it maintains.\" Owner references \"tell the control plane which objects are dependent on\nothers, and Kubernetes manages them automatically in most cases.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] [source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24]\n(Ch 6 §3)\n> ★ Fixed Point wording, recorded as canonical: **\"Ownership is exclusive; selection is\n> not.\"** The documentation is explicit that ownership \"is different from the labels and\n> selectors mechanism.\"\n\n**ownership chain** — Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod. \"The Deployment owns the Pod template\nand the update strategy. The ReplicaSet owns a replica count and enforces it. The Pods run.\"\n(Ch 6 §1, authorial synthesis over\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] + [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24])\n> ★ Fixed Point. **The single most consequential model in the chapter**: Bearings #1 A1\n> states that a reader who believes a Deployment owns Pods directly finds §4\n> incomprehensible, \"because the mechanism has nowhere to live.\" Six later chapters depend\n> on it.\n\n**overlapping selectors** — the documented hazard: \"be careful not to overlap with the\nselectors of other controllers, lest they try to adopt this Pod.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3)\n> The chapter's Logbook Entry establishes that it fails in **two** directions — adoption\n> when the collision exists, orphaning when you fix it by editing a selector — and that\n> \"neither direction announces itself as a configuration mistake,\" because the API validates\n> only each controller's *internal* agreement. ⚑ A full sidebar with **no retrieval path**;\n> Practice Q4 tests bare-Pod adoption, which is adjacent but not the same mechanism.\n\n### P\n\n**pause / resume (rollout)** — \"you can pause rollouts before you trigger one or more\nupdates, then resume when you are ready to apply the changes, which lets you apply multiple\nfixes in between pausing and resuming without triggering unnecessary rollouts.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §5)\n> ⚓ Worth Securing, verbatim: \"Pause before a batch of template edits. It is the difference\n> between one rollout and four, and the four are not independent: each one restarts the work\n> of the one before it.\"\n\n**Pod template** — a ReplicaSet is defined with \"a Pod template specifying the data of new\nPods it should create; when it needs to create a new Pod, it uses that template.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] The nesting rule: a Pod template has \"exactly the\nsame schema as a Pod, except that it is nested and carries no `apiVersion` or `kind` of its\nown.\" [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §1)\n> ⚑ **The nesting rule is cached only for DaemonSet.** The Deployment API reference says\n> only \"Template describes the pods that will be created.\" Chapter 6 attributes it visibly\n> rather than generalizing. **To state it as a general rule, cache\n> `k8s-api-replicaset-v1-*` / `k8s-api-deployment-v1-*`** (research gap 3).\n> **CLOSES Chapter 5's `pod-template` reservation.**\n\n**`progressDeadlineSeconds`** — \"the number of seconds to wait for the Deployment to progress\nbefore the system reports back that it has failed to progress,\" surfaced as a condition with\n`type: Progressing`, `status: \"False\"`, `reason: ProgressDeadlineExceeded`. Defaults to 600,\n\"and the controller keeps retrying regardless.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n> ⚠ It produces a **signal**. It does not roll anything back. Practice Q12's distractor C\n> exists for exactly that misreading.\n\n### R\n\n**`Recreate`** — \"all existing Pods are killed before new ones are created.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §4)\n> ⚠ **A supported strategy with a stated cost, not a mistake.** The chapter is explicit:\n> some applications cannot have two versions running at once — a schema migration the old\n> code cannot read, an exclusive lock, a single-instance license — and for those, \"downtime\n> is not a failure of the deployment strategy; it is the cost of correctness, taken\n> deliberately and scheduled. **Treating `Recreate` as always-wrong is its own trap.**\"\n> The near-miss worth recording: `maxSurge: 0` removes the *extra* Pods but not the\n> *overlap*. Only `Recreate` guarantees no overlap.\n\n**ReplicaSet** — \"A ReplicaSet's purpose is to maintain a stable set of replica Pods running\nat any given time; it is often used to guarantee the availability of a specified number of\nidentical Pods.\" [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §1, §2)\n> A ReplicaSet \"replaces Pods that are deleted or terminated for any reason, such as node\n> failure or disruptive node maintenance like a kernel upgrade\" — which a hand-created Pod\n> does not get.\n\n**ReplicaSet — `.spec.replicas`** — \"specifies how many Pods should run concurrently, and the\nReplicaSet creates or deletes its Pods to match that number,\" defaulting to 1. A ReplicaSet\n\"can be scaled up or down by simply updating the `.spec.replicas` field.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §1, §2)\n> ⚓ Worth Securing, verbatim and nominated as an exemplar: \"**Self-healing and scaling are\n> not two features. They are one loop, seeing the same kind of gap, taking the same action.**\n> If you can hold that, you have most of what this chapter teaches.\"\n> The loop cannot distinguish \"you asked for five and there are three\" from \"you asked for\n> three and one died.\" **That indistinguishability is the teaching.**\n\n**ReplicationController** — \"the legacy API for managing workloads that can scale\nhorizontally, superseded by the Deployment and ReplicaSet APIs; a Deployment that configures\na ReplicaSet is now the recommended way to set up replication.\" ReplicaSets are its\nsuccessors, \"serving the same purpose and behaving similarly, except that a\nReplicationController does not support set-based selector requirements.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicationcontroller-2026-08-24]\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §1)\n> Recognition depth only. ⚑ The snapshot is truncated and its \"Alternatives\" section is not\n> cached; the set-based claim is correctly sourced to the ReplicaSet page instead.\n\n**resource (API sense)** — \"an endpoint in the Kubernetes API that stores a collection of API\nobjects of a certain kind; the built-in `pods` resource contains a collection of Pod\nobjects.\" [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n> Defined here for the first time in the book, six chapters after the reader started using\n> `kubectl get <resource>`. Needed before *custom* resource can mean anything.\n\n**revision** — \"created when a rollout is triggered, and a new revision is created **if and\nonly if** the Deployment's Pod template (`.spec.template`) is changed. Other updates, such as\nscaling the Deployment, do not create a revision.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §5)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"A revision is created if and only if `.spec.template` changes. Scaling\n> does not create a revision.\"**\n> The mechanism that makes the rule inevitable: **a revision *is* a ReplicaSet.** \"Each new\n> ReplicaSet updates the revision of the Deployment,\" and a new ReplicaSet is only created\n> when the template changes. Scaling changes a number on a ReplicaSet you already have.\n> ⚠ Corollary that catches people: `kubectl rollout undo` will not reverse a scale.\n\n**`revisionHistoryLimit`** — \"the number of old ReplicaSets to retain to allow rollback,\"\ndefaulting to **10**. Setting it to zero \"cleans up all old ReplicaSets with zero replicas,\nwith the consequence that a new rollout **cannot be undone**, since its revision history has\nbeen cleaned up.\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §5)\n> ⚠ The old ReplicaSets **are** the history. Deleting them removes the mechanism, not merely\n> the display. There is a defensible reason to set it to zero in a GitOps environment where\n> previous state lives elsewhere; \"there is no defensible reason to do it because the\n> `kubectl get rs` output was untidy.\"\n\n**rollback (Deployment sense)** — not undoing an edit and not restoring a backup: \"setting\nthe Pod template to a previous revision's value and letting the same rolling update run in\nthe other direction: same two ReplicaSets, same `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`, same\nreadiness gate, opposite direction of travel.\"\n(Ch 6 §5, authorial synthesis over [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23])\n> The ReplicaSet holding the old template \"has been riding at anchor the whole time with\n> nobody aboard, which is exactly why the operation is fast and exactly why deleting the\n> revision history removes the ability to do it.\"\n> ⚑ **The word \"rollback\" attaches to three different mechanisms in this book.** Ch 6 §5\n> flags both others by name: Helm rollback (Ch 14 §5) and a third at Ch 15 §5. Ch 14 and\n> Ch 15 must not let the word drift.\n\n**rolling update** — \"You declare the new state of the Pods by updating the Pod template on\nthe Deployment. A new ReplicaSet is created, and the Deployment manages moving Pods from the\nold ReplicaSet to the new one at a controlled rate.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §4)\n> **Two ReplicaSets exist simultaneously**, one shrinking and one growing, both owned by the\n> same Deployment, each doing nothing more exotic than holding a count and closing gaps.\n> \"A reader who has the ownership chain finds the rolling update obvious. A reader who does\n> not finds it magic.\"\n\n**`kubectl rollout` (the verb surface)** — valid resource types are deployments, daemonsets\nand statefulsets. Six subcommands: `status` (show the status of the rollout), `history` (view\nrollout history), `undo` (undo a previous rollout), `pause` (mark the provided resource as\npaused), `resume` (resume a paused resource), `restart` (restart a resource).\n[source: k8s-docs-kubectl-rollout-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §5)\n> ⚑ `kubectl rollout status` is the only one of the six with no question behind it.\n\n### S\n\n**selector (workload `.spec.selector`)** — a label selector \"using the labels that identify\npotential Pods to acquire.\" A controller's Pods \"are whichever Pods match. Not the Pods it\nmade. The ones that match, right now, whoever made them.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"A controller's Pods are the Pods matching its selector. Membership is a\n> query, not a list.\"**\n> Machinery is Ch 4's, unchanged: set-based requirements are expressed through\n> `matchExpressions`; `matchLabels` is the equality-shaped shorthand, \"equivalent to\n> `matchExpressions` with the operator `In`\"; Job, Deployment, ReplicaSet and DaemonSet\n> support both. [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]\n\n**selector–template agreement** ⚠ — in a ReplicaSet, \"`.spec.template.metadata.labels`\n**must** match `spec.selector` or the object will be rejected by the API.\" Same for a\nDeployment and for a DaemonSet.\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §3)\n> **The API rejects it outright** rather than accepting it and reconciling toward it,\n> \"because what you wrote has no reachable steady state at all\" — the runaway is the\n> *reason* for the validation, not its outcome.\n> ⚑ The chapter's outline assumed a live runaway; all three sources say rejection. Stage 6\n> confirmed. Bearings #1 item 3 and Practice Q3 are aligned to the rejection reading, and\n> Bearings #1 A3 credits the runaway intuition as correct-but-superseded.\n\n**StatefulSet** — \"runs a group of Pods and maintains a sticky identity for each of them,\nwhich is useful for managing applications that need persistent storage or a stable, unique\nnetwork identity.\" Like a Deployment it \"manages Pods based on an identical container spec,\nbut unlike a Deployment it maintains a sticky identity for each Pod, and **these Pods are\ncreated from the same spec but are not interchangeable: each has a persistent identifier\nthat it maintains across any rescheduling**.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §6)\n> ★ Fixed Point: **\"The property that distinguishes a StatefulSet from a Deployment is\n> whether the Pods are interchangeable, not whether the application writes to disk.\"**\n> 🪝 The test, verbatim: \"**if I destroyed this Pod and a differently-named Pod appeared in\n> its place, would anything be broken?** If the answer is no, it is a Deployment, disk or\n> no disk.\"\n> ⚑ B1 trap **#21**, `[source]`-backed (`domain-analysis.md:524`).\n\n**StatefulSet — ordinal identity** — \"for a StatefulSet with N replicas, each Pod is assigned\nan integer ordinal unique across the set — **by default**, from 0 through N−1 — and each Pod\nderives its hostname from the StatefulSet's name and its own ordinal. The pattern is\n`$(statefulset name)-$(ordinal)`.\" [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §6)\n> ⚑ \"By default\" is load-bearing: the start ordinal is configurable. Stage 6 caught its\n> omission and it was restored.\n> The identity is a mechanism **layered over** selection, not expressed by it — which is\n> what Practice Q17 grades. The controller also adds a per-Pod name label,\n> `statefulset.kubernetes.io/pod-name`.\n\n**StatefulSet — storage follows the identity** — \"for each volume claim template defined in a\nStatefulSet, each Pod receives one PersistentVolumeClaim, and **the same claim will be bound\nto that Pod throughout its lifecycle**; when the Pod is rescheduled onto a different node,\nits mounts follow.\" [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §6)\n> Figure note that must survive any redraw: in `ch06-fig05` the volume is drawn attached to\n> `db-0`, \"which is a slot that Pods pass through\" — **not** to a Pod.\n\n**StatefulSet — ordering guarantees** — \"Pods are created sequentially in order from 0 to\nN−1 and terminated in reverse order from N−1 to 0, and before a scaling operation is applied\nto a Pod, all of its predecessors must be Running and Ready.\"\n[source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §6)\n\n**stuck rollout** — a Deployment that \"gets stuck trying to deploy its newest ReplicaSet\nwithout ever completing.\" Named causes: **readiness probe failures**, image pull errors,\ninsufficient quota, insufficient permissions, limit ranges, and application runtime\nmisconfiguration. [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] (Ch 6 §4)\n> 🪝 Snag, verbatim: \"A stalled rollout is not a failed one… Your users see nothing. Your\n> dashboard sees nothing. `kubectl get deployments` shows a ready count that has stopped\n> moving. **Nothing will page you unless you asked something to.**\"\n> ⚠ \"Failed\" implies something ended; \"stalled\" means something is still waiting for you.\n\n### W\n\n**what makes a rolling update safe (not merely gradual)** — availability. A new Pod that\nnever reports ready never becomes available; a rollout that cannot accumulate available new\nPods cannot remove old ones without breaching the `maxUnavailable` floor, \"so it doesn't.\n**The rollout stalls instead of taking down the service.**\"\n(Ch 6 §4, authorial synthesis over\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24] + [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23])\n> **This is where Chapter 5's probes stop being a health-checking feature and become a\n> release-safety mechanism.** Ch 5 said a Pod that never reports ready never receives\n> traffic; Ch 6 states the larger consequence: **a *version* that never reports ready never\n> replaces the version that works.**\n> The register line worth preserving: \"The relief comes aboard before the old watch is\n> dismissed, and nobody is dismissed until the relief has actually reported fit.\"\n\n**workload resource** ★ — \"you don't need to manage each Pod directly. Instead, you use\nworkload resources that manage a set of Pods on your behalf, and these resources configure\ncontrollers that make sure the right number of the right kind of Pod are running, to match\nthe state you specified.\" [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §1)\n> **CLOSES Chapter 5's reservation.** Ch 5 named it only because Pod disposability forces it\n> to exist.\n> ⚓ Worth Securing, verbatim: \"If you find yourself writing a bare Pod manifest for anything\n> other than a one-off experiment, you have picked the wrong object. The question to ask is\n> not 'how do I run this container' but '**what should stay true about this container**.'\"\n\n**workload-resource decision (the four questions, in order)** — does the work end? → does it\nrepeat on a schedule? → must it run on every node? → are the Pods interchangeable?\n(Ch 6 §7, `ch06-fig04`)\n> ⚠ **All three of §7's traps share one root cause: people choose a workload resource by\n> what the application *is* rather than by how its Pods need to be *managed*.** Running the\n> tree in order makes all three unreachable, because it asks about the work before it asks\n> about the software. All three are `[source]`-backed B1 traps (#21, #22, #23).\n> The Exam Alert names this figure \"the single highest-value artifact in this chapter.\"\n\n---\n\n## Tier 2 — Partial at Chapter 6 (named, defining chapter elsewhere)\n\n**API-server aggregation** — characterized, not defined: the other route to a custom API,\nwhich \"offers more flexibility at the cost of writing and operating more of the API\nmachinery yourself.\" [source: k8s-docs-custom-resources-2026-08-23] (Ch 6 §8)\n→ ⚑ **No owner chapter assigned by B2.** Ch 6's scoping — \"CRD is the common path,\naggregation is the rarer one, and knowing that both exist is enough\" — is correct for\nassociate tier, so this may never need an owner. **Record the decision either way.**\n\n**EndpointSlice** — used twice, source-tagged, undefined. (Ch 6 Bearings #1 A5)\n→ **Ch 9.** Reserved since Chapter 3 §2. ⚑ This is a *depth escalation on a reserved term*:\nit now appears inside a graded answer key carrying a source tag. **Fix: add\n`*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — EndpointSlice]*`, or cut to \"the Service's endpoint objects.\"**\n\n**volume claim template** — named in the StatefulSet storage sentence, never defined, and\n⚑ **the only deferred term in Chapter 6 without a cross-bearing.** (Ch 6 §6)\n→ **Ch 11 §4.** Every other deferral in the chapter carries a pointer. Cheapest fix in the\nchapter, and the same omission-not-policy shape Chapter 5's `projected volume` had.\n\n---\n\n## Tier 3 — Reserved by Chapter 6 (named, deferred, owner recorded)\n\n| Term | Reserved to | Surfaced at | Bearing present? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| headless Service | Ch 9 §5 | Ch 6 §6 | ✅ — ⚑ but see the casing note below |\n| PersistentVolume / PersistentVolumeClaim | Ch 11 §4 | Ch 6 §6 | ✅ |\n| StorageClass / access modes / provisioning path | Ch 11 §4 | Ch 6 §6 | ✅ — named as a deliberate deferral, which is the right handling |\n| blue/green · canary · A/B | Ch 15 §4 | Ch 6 §4 | ✅ — the chapter's only mention, inside the single authorized bearing |\n| metrics-server | Ch 13 | Ch 6 §2, §8 | ⚑ **two different §-numbers for the same component, in one chapter** (§2 → Ch 13 §4, §8 → Ch 13 §2) |\n| Ingress / ingress controller | Ch 10 §4 | Ch 6 §8 | ✅ |\n| RBAC | Ch 12 §2 | Ch 6 §8, Q18 | ⚑ no bearing at ch-06:981 |\n| `nodeSelector` / node affinity | **Ch 7 — already defined** | Ch 6 §7, Voyage Ahead | ✅ at the Voyage Ahead; none in §7's DaemonSet paragraph (optional) |\n| CNI plugin | Ch 9 | Ch 6 §7 | ✅ — ⚑ Ch 6 says `Ch 9 §7`, Ch 2 says `Ch 9 §1` |\n\n### ⚑ One casing decision the glossary needs before Chapter 9\n\n`ch-06:870` writes **`Headless Service`** and **`headless Service`** in the same sentence.\nChapter 9 will set the canonical form for the rest of the book.\n\n**Recommendation: lowercase `headless Service`** — the word is an adjective modifying a\nresource kind, which matches this ledger's existing treatment of `custom resource`,\n`cluster DNS` and `workload resource`, and matches upstream's dominant usage. Author call;\nrecorded here so Chapter 9 inherits a decision rather than a coin toss.\n\n=== END APPEND ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/workload-resource.md ===\n# Concept: Workload resources, and the decision between them\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §1, §7 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Closes:** Chapter 5's reservation — named there, treatment deferred here.\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> You don't need to manage each Pod directly. Instead, you use **workload resources that\n> manage a set of Pods on your behalf**, and these resources configure controllers that\n> make sure the right number of the right kind of Pod are running, to match the state you\n> specified. [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]\n\nThat sentence is the whole chapter in miniature. Everything else is unpacking.\n\n## The reframe this shard exists to install\n\nThe Pod — the object Chapter 5 spent its entire length on — is one you will almost never\ncreate directly. Not because Pods are unimportant; they are still the only thing that\nactually runs. **Being the thing that gets created *for* you is what a Pod is for.**\n\nA ReplicaSet \"replaces Pods that are deleted or terminated for any reason, such as node\nfailure or disruptive node maintenance like a kernel upgrade\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. A Pod you created by hand does not get that.\n\n> ⚓ **Worth Securing (verbatim):** If you find yourself writing a bare Pod manifest for\n> anything other than a one-off experiment, you have picked the wrong object. The question\n> to ask is not \"how do I run this container\" but **\"what should stay true about this\n> container.\"**\n\n## The decision — four questions, in order\n\n`ch06-fig04-workload-resource-decision-tree`. The Exam Alert calls it \"the single\nhighest-value artifact in this chapter.\"\n\n1. **Does the work end?**\n   - yes → does it repeat on a schedule? → **CronJob** / **Job**\n   - no → continue\n2. **Must it run on every node?** → yes → **DaemonSet**\n3. **Are the Pods interchangeable?** → yes → **Deployment** (which manages a ReplicaSet) ·\n   no → **StatefulSet**\n\n**The question order is the pedagogy.** The first question is about the shape of the work,\nnot the nature of the software. That ordering is what makes the three traps below\nunreachable.\n\nThe documentation offers the same guidance from the ReplicaSet's side: use a Job for Pods\nexpected to terminate on their own; a DaemonSet for Pods providing a machine-level function\nsuch as machine monitoring or machine logging; and a Deployment when you want ReplicaSets,\n\"since Deployments own and manage them for you\" [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n## ⚠ The three traps and their single root cause\n\n> **People choose a workload resource by what the application *is*, rather than by how its\n> Pods need to be *managed*.** Every one of the following is that mistake wearing a\n> different coat.\n\n| Trap | B1 # | Correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"It's a database, so it needs a StatefulSet\" | **#21** `[source]` | The criterion is interchangeability, not the software category |\n| \"I need six copies, so I'll use a DaemonSet\" | **#22** `[source]` | A DaemonSet expresses *per node*, not a number |\n| \"Job and CronJob both run scheduled work\" | **#23** `[source]` | A Job runs once. A CronJob *makes* Jobs |\n\nAll three are `[source]`-backed in B1 (`domain-analysis.md:524–526`), so the chapter's\nprevalence framing is licensed under B2's rule at `chapter-lineup:184`. **Recorded here\nbecause Stage 13 flagged it as unverified and it is now verified.**\n\n## Related\n\n[[deployment]] · [[statefulset]] · [[daemonset]] · [[job]] · [[cronjob]] · [[pod]] ·\n[[ownership-chain]] · [[control-loop]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/ownership-chain.md ===\n# Concept: The ownership chain — Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §1 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Depth here:** the structural key to §4, §5, and six later chapters.\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **The chain is Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod. The Deployment owns the Pod template and\n> the update strategy. The ReplicaSet owns a replica count and enforces it. The Pods run.**\n\nThree layers, not two. \"You create a Deployment; the Deployment creates a **ReplicaSet**;\nthe ReplicaSet creates Pods in the background\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n\n## The nuance that makes the Fixed Point survive §4\n\n`.spec.replicas` appears on **both** objects — optional and defaulting to 1 on the\nDeployment [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24], and on the ReplicaSet\nspecifying \"how many Pods should run concurrently,\" also defaulting to 1\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n> **Same field name, two altitudes: one is where you express the wish, the other is where\n> the wish is enforced.**\n\nThe chapter states the simple version, then discloses the full picture in the same\nparagraph — \"the number is written twice, and the Deployment is the author of the second\ncopy.\" That is the Order/Truth pattern executed correctly, and it is why the Fixed Point\ndoes not break when §4 shows two ReplicaSets at once.\n\n⚑ **Hedge preserved from Stage 6 W-4.** §1 says the Deployment sets the count \"on the\nReplicaSet it currently considers current — §4 shows what happens when there are two.\"\nDuring a rollout the controller sets counts on **all** active ReplicaSets (proportional\nscaling) [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]. Proportional scaling itself\nis out of scope for this book; the hedge is what keeps §1 from contradicting §4.\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch06-fig01-deployment-replicaset-pod-ownership` — the caption is load-bearing:\n**\"intent flows DOWN · existence is reported back UP.\"** The template and strategy are\ndrawn in the top box, the count in the middle box. Any redraw must keep them separated,\nbecause §4's mechanism has nowhere to live otherwise.\n\n## ⚑ The misconception this shard exists to kill\n\n**\"A Deployment owns Pods directly.\"** Bearings #1 A1 names it as the most consequential\nwrong answer in the chapter and says why: a rolling update works by having **two**\nReplicaSets alive at once, one shrinking and one growing, with the Deployment holding both.\n\"Delete the middle layer from your mental model and the mechanism has nowhere to live.\"\n\nThe mirror-image error is treating the ReplicaSet as **passive** — a bookkeeping record of\nwhat the Deployment did. It is a running controller that creates and deletes Pods to reach\nits own `.spec.replicas` [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. **The Deployment does\nnot touch Pods at all.**\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| **Ch 14** | A Helm chart's job is to template these objects — Ch 6 §1 bears to `Ch 14 §2` |\n| **Ch 15** | The delivery tool acts on these objects, not on Pods |\n\n## Related\n\n[[deployment]] · [[replicaset]] · [[pod]] · [[owner-reference]] · [[rolling-update]] ·\n[[revision]] · [[spec]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/deployment.md ===\n# Concept: Deployment\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §1, §4, §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A Deployment **manages a set of Pods to run an application workload, usually one that\n> doesn't maintain state**, and it provides **declarative updates for Pods *and\n> ReplicaSets***. [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23]\n\n⚑ **Retain \"and ReplicaSets.\"** It is the half readers skim and the structural key to §4\nand §5. A Deployment is not a Pod manager; it is a *ReplicaSet* manager whose ReplicaSets\nmanage Pods.\n\n## Why you reach for it\n\nThe recommendation is the documentation's own, and it is a *recommendation*, not a\npopularity claim: \"we recommend using Deployments instead of directly using ReplicaSets,\"\nand \"usually, you define a Deployment and let that Deployment manage ReplicaSets\nautomatically\" — you \"may never need to manipulate a ReplicaSet object directly\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n⚑ **Do not restore the frequency form.** Stage 6 F-4 caught \"the most common workload\nresource is the Deployment\" as an untagged ecosystem-usage claim; the fix was the\nrecommendation phrasing above. Nothing in the corpus ranks workload-resource usage.\n\n## What it owns\n\n- **The Pod template** — what a replacement *is*\n- **The update strategy** — how replacements are *made*\n- **The count you write**, which it then sets on the ReplicaSet it considers current\n\n## What it is for\n\nA Deployment \"is a good fit for managing a stateless application workload where **any Pod\nin the Deployment is interchangeable** and can be replaced if needed\"\n[source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23].\n\n**That assumption is what makes \"a count plus a template\" a sufficient description of the\nwhole workload.** If any Pod can stand in for any other, \"three of these\" says everything\nthere is to say. `statefulset.md` is what happens when it doesn't hold.\n\n## The three things it does that a ReplicaSet cannot\n\n1. **Rolling updates** — two ReplicaSets at once, at a controlled rate → `rolling-update.md`\n2. **Revision history** — old ReplicaSets retained as the rollback mechanism → `revision.md`\n3. **Strategy selection** — `RollingUpdate` (default) or `Recreate` → `recreate-strategy.md`\n\n## Related\n\n[[ownership-chain]] · [[replicaset]] · [[rolling-update]] · [[recreate-strategy]] ·\n[[revision]] · [[statefulset]] · [[workload-resource]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/replicaset.md ===\n# Concept: ReplicaSet — the control loop you can watch\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §1, §2, §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Discharges:** `control-loop.md`'s Chapter 6 obligation — the theme's first *planned*\nretrieval, and B2's \"second beat of the book's spine.\"\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A ReplicaSet's purpose is to **maintain a stable set of replica Pods running at any given\n> time**; it is often used to guarantee the availability of a specified number of identical\n> Pods. [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]\n\nIt is defined by three things: a **selector** (how to identify Pods it can acquire), a\n**replica count**, and a **Pod template** (the data of new Pods it should create). \"When it\nneeds to create a new Pod, it uses that template.\"\n\n## Chapter 3's loop with the numbers filled in\n\n| Chapter 3's abstraction | A ReplicaSet's instance |\n|---|---|\n| desired state (`spec`) | `.spec.replicas` |\n| current state | how many matching Pods actually exist |\n| the action | create or delete Pods until they agree |\n\n[source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]\n\n**Chapter 6 §2 opens by quoting Chapter 3's own forward-bearing text back to the reader** —\n\"Chapter 3 promised you a control loop you could watch working in real time. This is it\" —\nwhich is the verbatim-handoff discipline `spec.md` identifies as the model for every\ndeferred-term payoff in the book.\n\n## ⚓ Worth Securing — the single most valuable sentence in the section\n\n> **Self-healing and scaling are not two features. They are one loop, seeing the same kind\n> of gap, taking the same action.** If you can hold that, you have most of what this\n> chapter teaches.\n\nDelete a Pod and the gap is 1, appearing because something died. Scale from 3 to 5 and the\ngap is 2, appearing because you asked. **The loop cannot tell the difference.** Bearings #1\nQ2 grades precisely this, and its key insists on the negative: *nothing was triggered.*\n\n## ✅ It instances `control-loop.md`'s COMMON shape, which repairs a blur\n\nThe shard's Fixed Point says a controller \"usually acts by asking the API server to change\nsomething, **not by doing the thing itself**.\" Chapter 6 §2 states it explicitly: \"the\ncontroller does not create that Pod itself… **The ReplicaSet controller writes a wish to\nthe API server and something else does the work**\"\n[source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n\nChapter 5's ledger recorded a near-miss — the kubelet presented as canonical when it is\nChapter 3's *uncommon* direct-control case. With Chapter 6 §2 and Chapter 7 §7, the book now\nhas two clean instances of the common shape against one uncommon. **The blur is repaired\nwithout editing Chapter 5.**\n\n## The never-stable design position, now with teeth\n\n\"Your cluster could be changing at any point as work happens and control loops automatically\nfix failures, which means that potentially your cluster never reaches a stable state; and as\nlong as the controllers are running and able to make useful changes, it doesn't matter\nwhether the overall state is stable\" [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n\n> **Kubernetes is not trying to arrive anywhere. It is trying to keep closing gaps.**\n\n## Scaling, and the autoscaler (one paragraph, deliberately)\n\n`kubectl scale` updates \"the size of the specified deployment\"\n[source: k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23]. When the number isn't yours, it is usually a\n**HorizontalPodAutoscaler**: \"an API resource plus a controller, running in the control\nplane, that periodically adjusts the desired scale of its target to match observed metrics\nsuch as average CPU utilization,\" implemented as \"a control loop that runs **intermittently\nrather than continuously**\" [source: k8s-docs-hpa-2026-08-24]. A ReplicaSet can be an HPA\ntarget directly, though in practice you point one at a Deployment.\n\n⚑ Metrics sources are Chapter 13's; the autoscaling landscape is Chapter 17's. Chapter 6\ncorrectly stops here. **But the HPA is taught, summarized, cited in an answer key, and never\ntested** — see the chapter's question-coverage AUTHOR-REVIEW.\n\n⚑ **Two §-numbers, one component.** §2 bears metrics-server to `Ch 13 §4`; §8 bears it to\n`Ch 13 §2`. Chapter 6 disagrees with itself. Resolve, or drop both `§`s.\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| **Ch 7** | The Pod this loop creates still has to be placed, and sometimes can't be. ✅ Ch 7 retrieves this three times |\n| **Ch 9** | \"This churn is exactly why something needs a stable name\" — ⚑ pinned at `Ch 9 §1`, now **three-deep** |\n| **Ch 13** | metrics-server as the HPA's input |\n\n## Related\n\n[[control-loop]] · [[ownership-chain]] · [[deployment]] · [[selector-template-agreement]] ·\n[[owner-reference]] · [[pod-lifetime]] · [[spec]] · [[status]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/selector-template-agreement.md ===\n# Concept: How a controller knows its own — selection\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Discharges:** `label-selector.md`'s Chapter 6 obligation — \"a ReplicaSet knows which Pods\nare *its* Pods by selector.\" First of that shard's four uses to be collected.\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **A controller's Pods are the Pods matching its selector. Membership is a query, not a\n> list. Because it is a query, the Pod template's labels must satisfy the selector, and the\n> API rejects any workload object where they don't.**\n\nA ReplicaSet does not track its Pods by name and holds no list. It has a `.spec.selector`,\n\"using the labels that identify potential Pods to acquire\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]. **Its Pods are whichever Pods match — not the\nPods it made. The ones that match, right now, whoever made them.**\n\n## The machinery is Chapter 4's, unchanged\n\nSet-based requirements are expressed through `matchExpressions`; `matchLabels` is the\nequality-shaped shorthand, \"equivalent to `matchExpressions` with the operator `In`\"; Job,\nDeployment, ReplicaSet and DaemonSet support both\n[source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n\n⚑ Consistent with `label-selector.md`'s \"exact equivalence; `matchLabels` is shorthand, not\na weaker feature.\" The Chapter 6 wording is the post-Stage-6 (W-2) correction; the earlier\ndraft's \"through both\" wrongly attributed set-based support to `matchLabels`.\n\n⚑ **Chapter 6 never names `ch04-fig03-labels-selectors-join`**, which the outline\nspecifically asked it to reference \"as a spacing-effect retrieval at zero cost.\" A free\nretrieval on the exact theme Chapter 6 was told to retrieve. One clause recovers it.\n\n## ⚠ Selector–template agreement — the API says no\n\nThe template's labels **must** match the selector \"or the object will be rejected by the\nAPI.\" Stated in the same words for ReplicaSet\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24], Deployment\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24], and DaemonSet\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n\n**The runaway is the reason for the validation, not its outcome.** Create a Pod, fail to see\nit, notice the count is still short, create another, forever, with no terminating condition.\n\"It is one of the few validations Kubernetes performs by refusing the object outright rather\nthan accepting it and reconciling toward it, and the reason is simple: **what you wrote has\nno reachable steady state at all.**\"\n\n⚑ Two records for this shard. (1) The chapter's *outline* assumed a live runaway; all three\nsources say rejection, confirmed by Stage 6. Bearings #1 item 3 and Practice Q3 are aligned\nto the rejection reading, and **Bearings #1 A3 credits the runaway intuition as\ncorrect-but-superseded** rather than simply marking it wrong. (2) The earlier draft's\nscarcity superlative — \"nearly the only place in Kubernetes where the API says no\" — was\n**correctly dropped** (Stage 6 F-3); the corpus supplies three counterexamples\n(DaemonSet selector immutability, cross-namespace owner references, admission control).\n\n## Where the selector actually bites — adoption\n\nThe API stops you misconfiguring **one** controller. It does not stop two controllers from\nclaiming the same Pods, or a controller from claiming a Pod you made.\n\n\"If a Pod has no owner reference, or an owner reference that is not a controller, and it\nmatches the selector, it will be **immediately acquired**.\" A ReplicaSet \"is not limited to\nowning Pods produced by its own template,\" and if acquisition puts it over its desired\ncount, \"the newly acquired Pod is **immediately terminated**\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n> 🪝 **Snag (verbatim):** You create a debugging Pod with `app: web` on it, because that is\n> what the other Pods have. It is adopted by the ReplicaSet and killed within seconds.\n> Nothing errors. Nothing warns you. **Your Pod was simply surplus to a count somebody else\n> was holding.**\n\n## Overlapping selectors — two directions, one habit\n\nThe documented advice is blunt: \"be careful not to overlap with the selectors of other\ncontrollers, lest they try to adopt this Pod\" [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\nChapter 6's Logbook Entry establishes that it fails **both** ways, and that neither way\nannounces itself as a configuration mistake, because the API validates only each\ncontroller's *internal* agreement:\n\n1. **The collision** → adoption, then immediate termination of the surplus Pod.\n2. **The obvious fix** (editing one selector) → **orphaning**, which is why a DaemonSet\n   forbids the edit outright [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n\n> **The prevention is a single habit, and it costs nothing:** give every workload one label\n> that is genuinely unique to it, and never hand-write a selector by copying somebody\n> else's.\n\n⚑ **This sidebar has no retrieval path.** Practice Q4 tests bare-Pod adoption, which is\nadjacent but a different mechanism. A full §18.15 sidebar with nothing grading it.\n\n## The distinction this shard's other half depends on\n\n**Selection answers \"which Pods are mine right now.\" Ownership answers \"who is responsible\nfor this object.\" Ownership is exclusive; selection is not.** A Pod can satisfy any number\nof independent queries — a ReplicaSet's and a Service's, say — and none of them owns it by\ndoing so. Bearings #1 Q5 grades exactly that, and its key notes the documentation draws the\nsame distinction for EndpointSlices, which are both *selected by* a Service and *owned via*\nan owner reference [source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24].\n\nSee `owner-reference.md`.\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| **Ch 9** | A Service selects its backends with the same mechanism — a different controller reading the same labels. Holding the wrong version of *ownership is exclusive, selection is not* makes Services look like they are fighting the workload controller |\n| **Ch 12** | ⚑ `label-selector.md`'s EXCEPTION: RBAC names rather than selects. Chapter 6 does not disturb it |\n\n## Related\n\n[[label-selector]] · [[owner-reference]] · [[replicaset]] · [[daemonset]] ·\n[[kubernetes-object]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/owner-reference.md ===\n# Concept: Owner references, cascading deletion, and orphans\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §3 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Split from** `selector-template-agreement.md` deliberately — see below.\n\n## Why this is a separate shard\n\nBecause the chapter's own load-bearing sentence is a *distinction*:\n\n> **Ownership is exclusive; selection is not.**\n\nThe documentation states outright that ownership \"is different from the labels and selectors\nmechanism\" [source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24], and Bearings #1 Q5 grades the\ndistinction directly. One shard holding both mechanisms would let the fact that matters fall\nbetween its halves.\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A ReplicaSet is linked to its Pods via the Pods' **`metadata.ownerReferences`** field,\n> which specifies what resource the current object is owned by; all Pods acquired by a\n> ReplicaSet carry the owning ReplicaSet's identifying information there, and **it is\n> through this link that the ReplicaSet knows the state of the Pods it maintains.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24]\n\n\"Many objects in Kubernetes link to each other this way; owner references tell the control\nplane which objects are dependent on others, and Kubernetes manages them automatically in\nmost cases\" [source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24].\n\n## Cascading deletion\n\n\"Kubernetes checks for and deletes objects that no longer have owner references, like the\nPods left behind when you delete a ReplicaSet, in a process called **cascading deletion**,\nand **background cascading deletion is the default**\"\n[source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24].\n\nIn practice: delete the Deployment and its ReplicaSets and their Pods go too. \"To delete a\nReplicaSet and all of its Pods you just use `kubectl delete`; the garbage collector\nautomatically removes the dependent Pods by default\"\n[source: k8s-docs-replicaset-2026-08-24].\n\n⚑ **Not a periodic sweep.** Practice Q6's distractor D invents an hourly garbage-collection\nwindow; there isn't one.\n\n## Orphans\n\n\"Dependents that survive their owner are called **orphan** objects, and by default Kubernetes\ndeletes dependents rather than leaving them behind\"\n[source: k8s-docs-garbage-collection-2026-08-24].\n\n**The way you produce orphans by accident is by changing a selector**, so Pods a controller\nused to claim stop matching it and nothing is left holding them. That route is hazardous\nenough that a DaemonSet's `.spec.selector` \"can not be mutated\" once created, \"precisely\nbecause mutating a pod selector can lead to the unintentional orphaning of Pods\"\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n\n⚑ Curriculum-alignment marked this sub-objective (**D1.1-16**) **absent** from the audited\ndraft. It was added in revision and the shipped chapter covers it in four places. **The\nshipped chapter is 36/36 on sub-objectives; the audit's 35/36 predates the fix.**\n\n⚑ `--cascade=orphan` and finalizers are **deliberately out of scope** and return zero hits\nin the chapter. Do not add them to this shard; the outline set that boundary and the\ncurriculum audit confirmed it held.\n\n## Related\n\n[[selector-template-agreement]] · [[ownership-chain]] · [[replicaset]] · [[daemonset]] ·\n[[kubernetes-object]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/rolling-update.md ===\n# Concept: The rolling update, and the two bounds\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §4 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Depth here:** the chapter's densest section, and the payoff of a promise Chapter 5 made.\n\n## The mechanism (verbatim)\n\n> You declare the new state of the Pods by updating the Pod template on the Deployment. **A\n> new ReplicaSet is created**, and the Deployment manages **moving Pods from the old\n> ReplicaSet to the new one at a controlled rate.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23]\n\n**Two ReplicaSets exist simultaneously**, both owned by the same Deployment, each doing\nnothing more exotic than holding a count and closing gaps. The Deployment's entire\ncontribution is deciding, moment to moment, what those two counts should be.\n\n> A reader who has the ownership chain finds the rolling update obvious. A reader who does\n> not finds it magic.\n\n`RollingUpdate` is the **default** `.spec.strategy.type`\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **`RollingUpdate` is the default strategy. `Recreate` kills every old Pod before creating\n> any new one. `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable` both default to 25%.**\n>\n> The discriminator between the two bounds is the rounding, and it runs in opposite\n> directions: **surge rounds up, unavailable rounds down.** Both defaults therefore err\n> toward keeping more capacity online, **which is the sentence to memorize if you can only\n> keep one — you can rebuild both rules from it.**\n\n## The two bounds, verbatim\n\n| Field | What it bounds | Percentage rounds | Default |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **`maxSurge`** | \"the maximum number of Pods that can be created **over** the desired number of Pods\" | **up** | 25% |\n| **`maxUnavailable`** | \"the maximum number of Pods that can be **unavailable** during the update\" | **down** | 25% |\n\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]\n\n**They are opposite in kind, not two settings of one knob.** `maxSurge` is a ceiling on how\nmany Pods *exist*; `maxUnavailable` is a floor on how many are *usable*.\n\n> 🪢 **Mnemonic:** Surge is above the line. Unavailable is below it.\n\n**The zero-pair constraint:** \"neither one can be zero if the other is zero\" — because a\nrollout that may neither exceed the desired count nor drop below it has no legal move.\n`maxSurge: 0` with `maxUnavailable: 1` **is** legal, and is what you reach for on a\ncapacity-constrained cluster (Practice Q7).\n\n## ⚑ The worked example — 13, not 12\n\nTen replicas at defaults:\n\n| Field | 25% of 10 | Rounds | Absolute |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `maxSurge` | 2.5 | **up** | **3** → ceiling 13 |\n| `maxUnavailable` | 2.5 | **down** | **2** → floor 8 |\n\n**The outline states the ceiling as 12. It is 13.** Stage 6 confirmed ⌈2.5⌉ = 3 against both\nthe concept page and the API reference. The chapter uses 13 throughout, including `fig02`,\nBearings #2 Q1, and Practice Q8's independent six-replica case (ceiling 8, floor 5).\n**⚑ The outline's 12 must be corrected before it propagates to The Lodestar.**\n\n⚑ The worked-arithmetic block is a **table**, not a fenced ASCII figure. It was fenced in\ndraft-v1 and tripped the structural linter's figure-anchor check — the only FAIL in an\notherwise 30/30 pass. If the author prefers it rendered, it needs an anchor\n(`ch06-fig07-rolling-update-arithmetic`) and an `image-specs.md` entry. **Either way the two\nrounding rows must stay adjacent, so the asymmetry is visible at a glance.**\n\n## What makes it safe rather than merely gradual\n\n> Replacing ten broken Pods two at a time still ends with ten broken Pods; it just takes\n> longer.\n\nWhat makes it safe is **availability**. Kubernetes marks a Deployment as progressing when\nnew Pods \"become ready or available, where available means ready for at least\n`minReadySeconds`\" — which defaults to 0, \"meaning a Pod counts as available the moment it\nis ready\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24]. And *ready* is Chapter 5's\nword: a failing readiness probe removes the Pod's IP \"from the endpoints of all Services\nthat match the Pod\" [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23].\n\n> **A new Pod that never reports ready never becomes available. A rollout that cannot\n> accumulate available new Pods cannot remove old ones without breaching the\n> `maxUnavailable` floor. So it doesn't. The rollout stalls instead of taking down the\n> service.**\n\n**This is where Chapter 5's probes stop being a health-checking feature and become a\nrelease-safety mechanism.** Chapter 5 said a Pod that never reports ready never receives\ntraffic; Chapter 6 states the larger consequence: **a *version* that never reports ready\nnever replaces the version that works.** `chapter-05:858` forward-bears here for exactly\nthis; both halves of that seam are correct.\n\nThe register line worth preserving: \"The relief comes aboard before the old watch is\ndismissed, and nobody is dismissed until the relief has actually reported fit.\"\n\n## The stalled rollout\n\nReadiness failures are named among six documented causes of a Deployment getting \"stuck\ntrying to deploy its newest ReplicaSet without ever completing,\" alongside image pull\nerrors, insufficient quota, insufficient permissions, limit ranges, and application runtime\nmisconfiguration [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24].\n\n> 🪝 **Snag (verbatim):** A stalled rollout is not a failed one… Your users see nothing.\n> Your dashboard sees nothing. `kubectl get deployments` shows a ready count that has\n> stopped moving. **Nothing will page you unless you asked something to.**\n\n`.spec.progressDeadlineSeconds` (default **600**) surfaces a `Progressing` condition with\n`reason: ProgressDeadlineExceeded`, \"and the controller keeps retrying regardless.\" ⚠ **It\nproduces a signal. It does not roll anything back.**\n\n## Downstream obligations\n\n| Chapter | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| **Ch 13** | Diagnose a stuck rollout and identify **which of the six causes** fired. Ch 6 publishes the list and assigns the diagnosis explicitly |\n| **Ch 15** | **Blue/green, canary and A/B** — release *strategies* built on top of this mechanism. Ch 6's single authorized forward bearing names `Ch 15 §4` and is the chapter's only mention |\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch06-fig02-rolling-update-maxsurge-maxunavailable` — the caption is load-bearing: **\"the\ntwo bounds are opposite in kind.\"** The ceiling and floor must be drawn as *lines* the\npopulation moves between, not as two values of one setting. Practice Q7's distractor D\nexists for the misconception a single-knob drawing would install.\n\n## Related\n\n[[recreate-strategy]] · [[deployment]] · [[ownership-chain]] · [[probe]] · [[revision]] ·\n[[replicaset]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/recreate-strategy.md ===\n# Concept: `Recreate` — downtime chosen on purpose\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §4 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Split from** `rolling-update.md` because the discrimination is graded twice.\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> With `Recreate`, **all existing Pods are killed before new ones are created.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23]\n\nIt is one of two values of `.spec.strategy.type`; `RollingUpdate` is the default.\n\n## ⚠ It is a supported strategy with a stated cost, not a mistake\n\nThe chapter is explicit, and this is the part most treatments get wrong:\n\n> Some applications genuinely cannot have two versions running at once: a schema migration\n> that the old code cannot read, an exclusive lock on a resource, a license that permits one\n> active instance. For those, **downtime is not a failure of the deployment strategy; it is\n> the cost of correctness, taken deliberately and scheduled.** Treating `Recreate` as\n> always-wrong is its own trap.\n\nPractice Q9 and Bearings #2 Q3 both grade this, and both keys insist on the *reason*: you\nchoose it for correctness, not for convenience.\n\n## ⚑ The near-miss this shard exists to hold\n\n**`maxSurge: 0` is not a substitute for `Recreate`.**\n\nWith `maxSurge: 0` and `maxUnavailable: 1`, no *extra* Pods are created — but old and new\nversions still coexist while the update walks through the fleet, which is exactly what a\nschema migration forbids [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24].\n\n> **`maxSurge: 0` removes the extra Pods. It does not remove the overlap. Only `Recreate`\n> guarantees no overlap.**\n\nPractice Q9's option C is the best distractor in the chapter precisely because it is\n*materially true* — `Recreate` does surge nothing, and people do reach for it on clusters\nwith no headroom. It is still the wrong reason. **A shard that folded `Recreate` into\n`rolling-update.md` would let this fall between the halves.**\n\n## The inverted risk\n\nQ9's option D asserts that `Recreate` \"avoids the need for readiness probes.\" It inverts the\nrisk: with `Recreate` you have **less** protection, not more, because there is no overlap\nwindow in which a failing new version can be caught before the old one is gone. `Recreate`\nis the one strategy where `rolling-update.md`'s safety property does not apply.\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch06-fig03-recreate-vs-rolling` — the caption is the argument: **\"the gap is the whole\npoint.\"** `Recreate` has a window in which nothing is serving, and \"that window is not a bug;\nit is the thing you chose.\" Any redraw must show the availability trace reaching **zero**.\n\n## Related\n\n[[rolling-update]] · [[deployment]] · [[probe]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/revision.md ===\n# Concept: Revisions, rollback, and what gets kept\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §5 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **A revision is created if and only if `.spec.template` changes. Scaling does not create a\n> revision.**\n>\n> The corollary catches people: `kubectl rollout undo` will not reverse a scale. There is no\n> revision to go back to, because scaling never made one.\n\nSource, in its own words: a revision \"is created when a rollout is triggered, and a new\nrevision is created **if and only if** the Deployment's Pod template (`.spec.template`) is\nchanged. Other updates, such as scaling the Deployment, do not create a revision\"\n[source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n\n## Why the rule works that way — the mechanism, not the rule\n\n**A revision *is* a ReplicaSet.** \"Each new ReplicaSet updates the revision of the\nDeployment,\" and a new ReplicaSet is only created when the template changes: a different\ntemplate is a different *kind* of Pod, which needs a different ReplicaSet to hold it.\nScaling changes a number on the ReplicaSet you already have. Nothing new was created, so\nnothing new was recorded.\n\n> **The history records *kinds of Pod*, not *states of Deployment*.**\n\nHold this and every other fact in the section follows, including why\n`revisionHistoryLimit: 0` removes the ability to undo.\n\n## What a rollback actually is\n\nNot undoing an edit, and not restoring a backup:\n\n> Setting the Pod template to a previous revision's value and letting the same rolling\n> update from §4 run in the other direction: **same two ReplicaSets, same `maxSurge` and\n> `maxUnavailable`, same readiness gate, opposite direction of travel.** The ReplicaSet\n> holding the old template has been riding at anchor the whole time with nobody aboard,\n> which is exactly why the operation is fast and exactly why deleting the revision history\n> removes the ability to do it.\n\n`kubectl rollout undo` rolls back to the previous revision; `--to-revision=<n>` targets a\nspecific one [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n\n⚑ **\"Rollback\" attaches to three different mechanisms in this book, and Chapter 6 flags\nboth others by name:** Helm rollback (`Ch 14 §5`) and a third at `Ch 15 §5`. **Chapters 14\nand 15 must not let the word drift** — this is the clearest pre-emptive terminology warning\nany chapter has issued.\n\n## What is kept\n\n`.spec.revisionHistoryLimit` is \"the number of old ReplicaSets to retain to allow rollback,\"\ndefaulting to **10**. Setting it to zero \"cleans up all old ReplicaSets with zero replicas,\nwith the consequence that a new rollout **cannot be undone**, since its revision history has\nbeen cleaned up\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-spec-fields-2026-08-24].\n\n⚠ **The old ReplicaSets *are* the history.** Deleting them removes the mechanism, not merely\nthe display — which is why the \"a revision is a ReplicaSet\" framing above is load-bearing\nrather than decorative. Practice Q3's option A is exactly this misreading.\n\n> There is a defensible reason to set it to zero in a GitOps environment where the previous\n> state lives somewhere else entirely. **There is no defensible reason to do it because the\n> `kubectl get rs` output was untidy.**\n\n## The verb surface — six subcommands, closed set\n\n`kubectl rollout` operates on deployments, daemonsets and statefulsets\n[source: k8s-docs-kubectl-rollout-2026-08-24]:\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `status` | Show the status of the rollout |\n| `history` | View rollout history |\n| `undo` | Undo a previous rollout |\n| `pause` | Mark the provided resource as paused |\n| `resume` | Resume a paused resource |\n| `restart` | Restart a resource |\n\n⚑ `kubectl rollout status` is the only one with no question behind it.\n\n**Pause and resume read as arbitrary until you know why they exist:** you can \"pause rollouts\nbefore you trigger one or more updates, then resume when you are ready to apply the changes,\nwhich lets you apply multiple fixes in between pausing and resuming without triggering\nunnecessary rollouts\" [source: k8s-docs-deployment-2026-08-23].\n\n> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Pause before a batch of template edits. It is the difference between\n> one rollout and four, **and the four are not independent: each one restarts the work of the\n> one before it.**\n\n## Related\n\n[[rolling-update]] · [[deployment]] · [[replicaset]] · [[recreate-strategy]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/statefulset.md ===\n# Concept: StatefulSet — when Pods are not interchangeable\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §6 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Closes:** Chapter 1's and Chapter 5's reservation.\n\n## The assumption it breaks\n\nEverything before §6 rests on one unnamed assumption. A Deployment \"is a good fit for\nmanaging a stateless application workload where **any Pod in the Deployment is\ninterchangeable** and can be replaced if needed\" [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23].\n\n**That is why a count plus a template is a sufficient description.** If any Pod can stand in\nfor any other, \"three of these\" says everything there is to say.\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A StatefulSet **runs a group of Pods and maintains a sticky identity for each of them**,\n> which is useful for managing applications that need persistent storage or a stable, unique\n> network identity. Like a Deployment, a StatefulSet manages Pods based on an identical\n> container spec, but unlike a Deployment it maintains a sticky identity for each Pod, and\n> **these Pods are created from the same spec but are not interchangeable: each has a\n> persistent identifier that it maintains across any rescheduling.**\n> [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24]\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **The property that distinguishes a StatefulSet from a Deployment is whether the Pods are\n> interchangeable, not whether the application writes to disk.**\n\n## 🪝 The misconception (verbatim) — B1 trap #21, `[source]`-backed\n\n> \"It writes to disk, so it needs a StatefulSet.\" **No.** A stateless web server can write\n> to disk (caches, temp files, uploaded images on a shared volume) and a Deployment's Pods\n> can mount volumes perfectly well. The question is never *does it write*. The question is:\n> **if I destroyed this Pod and a differently-named Pod appeared in its place, would\n> anything be broken?** If the answer is no, it is a Deployment, disk or no disk.\n\nIt **fails in both directions**: unnecessary machinery around stateless services that cache\nto disk, and genuinely identity-dependent workloads left in Deployments where a routine Pod\nreplacement quietly returns the wrong data.\n\n**The adjacent error, worth holding separately** (Practice Q13 option B): *\"only one instance\nmay run\"* is not the same claim as *\"the instances are not interchangeable.\"* A Deployment\nwith `replicas: 1` expresses the first; if overlap must be prevented even during a rollout,\nthe strategy field is where you say so — `Recreate`, not StatefulSet.\n\n## The identity is concrete\n\n**Ordinal and hostname.** \"For a StatefulSet with N replicas, each Pod is assigned an integer\nordinal unique across the set — **by default**, from 0 through N−1 — and each Pod derives its\nhostname from the StatefulSet's name and its own ordinal. The pattern is\n`$(statefulset name)-$(ordinal)`\" — so `web` gives `web-0`, `web-1`, `web-2`\n[source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24].\n\n⚑ **\"By default\" is load-bearing** — the start ordinal is configurable. Stage 6 (W-3) caught\nits omission; one word restored.\n\n**Storage follows the identity.** \"For each volume claim template defined in a StatefulSet,\neach Pod receives one PersistentVolumeClaim, and **the same claim will be bound to that Pod\nthroughout its lifecycle**; when the Pod is rescheduled onto a different node, its mounts\nfollow\" [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24].\n\n**Ordering is guaranteed, not incidental.** \"Pods are created sequentially in order from 0 to\nN−1 and terminated in reverse order from N−1 to 0, and before a scaling operation is applied\nto a Pod, all of its predecessors must be Running and Ready.\"\n\n## Figure\n\n`ch06-fig05-statefulset-vs-deployment-identity` — **the volume is drawn attached to `db-0`,\nnot to a Pod.** The caption says why: `db-0` is \"a slot that Pods pass through.\" Any redraw\nmust preserve that; it is the whole argument.\n\n⚑ **Design-brief note for the diagram pipeline, cut from the reader's prose in revision:**\ndeleting or scaling down a StatefulSet does **not** delete the associated volumes\n[source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24]. That is the cleanest available evidence for the\nfigure's requirement, and it belongs in the brief rather than the chapter (Chapter 11 owns\nthe reader-facing version).\n\n⚑ **Anchor/caption transposition, unfixed.** This anchor carries caption \"Figure 6.4\" while\n`ch06-fig04-workload-resource-decision-tree` carries \"Figure 6.5.\" Caption numbering is\ninternally consistent and every in-text reference matches the captions, so the **captions are\nload-bearing and the anchor IDs are the outlier.** Swapping the two `figNN` tokens requires\none simultaneous edit in the draft *and* `image-specs.md`, because the anchor is the join key.\n\n## Extended Analogy (nominated as a voice exemplar)\n\nThe watch rotation vs. the pilot who knows this harbor. Preserved because the closing clause\nconverts the metaphor into an asymmetric-risk rule:\n\n> Both are legitimate ways to staff a task. **Confusing them costs you in exactly one\n> direction:** treat interchangeable crew as irreplaceable and you carry unnecessary\n> machinery; treat irreplaceable crew as interchangeable and you lose data.\n\n## Two loops left open on purpose\n\nChapter 6 tells the reader outright that both deferrals are deliberate rather than\nomissions — the correct Order/Truth handling, and worth copying:\n\n| Deferred | Owner | Bearing? |\n|---|---|---|\n| PersistentVolume, PVC, StorageClass, access modes, provisioning | **Ch 11 §4** | ✅ |\n| Headless Service — \"StatefulSets currently require a Headless Service to be responsible for the network identity of the Pods, and **you are responsible for creating it**\" [source: k8s-docs-statefulset-2026-08-24] | **Ch 9 §5** | ✅ |\n| `volume claim template` itself | **Ch 11** | ⚑ **NO BEARING** — the only deferred term in the chapter without one |\n\n⚑ **Casing:** `ch-06:870` writes `Headless Service` and `headless Service` in one sentence.\nRecommend lowercase `headless Service`; Chapter 9 sets the canonical form.\n\n## ⚑ A published cross-bearing that points at the wrong section\n\n`chapter-01:436` reads *\"see Ch 6 §3 — StatefulSets and stable identity.\"* StatefulSet is\n**§6**. The outline recorded §3 as pinned by `chapter-04:688` and therefore immovable — **but\nChapter 4's re-draft (`73fd066`) dropped section numbers from all three of its Ch 6 pointers,\nso that pin no longer exists.** The fix is unchanged (`§3` → `§6`, one token, in\nchapter-01), but the reasoning recorded in the outline is stale and will mislead the next\nsession.\n\n## Related\n\n[[deployment]] · [[workload-resource]] · [[pod-lifetime]] · [[recreate-strategy]] ·\n[[selector-template-agreement]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/daemonset.md ===\n# Concept: DaemonSet — one per node\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §7 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A DaemonSet **ensures that all (or some) nodes run a copy of a Pod. As nodes are added to\n> the cluster, Pods are added to them; as nodes are removed, those Pods are garbage\n> collected.** Deleting the DaemonSet cleans up the Pods it created.\n> [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24]\n\n> Read the first clause again, because the whole resource is in it: *as nodes are added, Pods\n> are added to them.* You never revisit the DaemonSet when the cluster grows. **The order was\n> written about the station, not about the number of hands standing it.**\n\n## ★ Fixed Point (verbatim — do not reword)\n\n> **One Pod per eligible node, added automatically as nodes join. The count is a consequence\n> of the cluster, not a setting.**\n\n## ⚑ The hedged form is mandatory\n\n**No cached sentence states that a DaemonSet has no `replicas` field.** The claim rests on\ntwo facts that together entail it:\n\n1. \"The controller creates a Pod for each eligible node\" [source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24]\n2. Horizontal autoscaling \"does not apply to objects that can't be scaled,\" and the\n   documentation's own example of such an object **is a DaemonSet** [source: k8s-docs-hpa-2026-08-24]\n\nThe chapter's prose, its §7 Fixed Point, and its Chapter Summary **all** use the hedged form\n— *the count is a consequence of node eligibility* — rather than asserting field absence.\n**Do not strengthen it** until the DaemonSet API reference is cached (research gap **G-6A**).\n\n## What determines the count\n\n\"If you specify a node selector or node affinity in the template, the DaemonSet controller\ncreates Pods on nodes matching it; if you specify neither, it creates Pods on all nodes\"\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n\n## 🪝 The misconception (verbatim) — B1 trap #22, `[source]`-backed\n\n> \"I need six copies for capacity, so I'll use a DaemonSet.\" A DaemonSet **does not express\n> a number.** It creates a Pod for each eligible node, so you get however many nodes are\n> eligible: possibly three, possibly sixty, and different tomorrow. If you want six, you want\n> a Deployment with `replicas: 6`. Use a DaemonSet when the requirement is genuinely *per\n> host*, which usually means **the Pod is doing something to or about the machine it is\n> sitting on.**\n\n## The purpose, and where it comes back\n\n\"A DaemonSet defines Pods that provide facilities local to nodes, and each Pod performs a job\nsimilar to a system daemon on a classic Unix or POSIX server\"\n[source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]. Typical uses: a cluster storage daemon, a\nlog-collection daemon, a node-monitoring daemon, on every node\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24].\n\n| Instance | Owner | Bearing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cluster networking plugins ship as DaemonSets | **Ch 9** | ⚑ Ch 6 says `§7`; Ch 2 says `Ch 9 §1` for CNI |\n| Node-level log agents | **Ch 18 §3** | ⚑ collides with Ch 5's `Ch 18 §3` |\n\n## Selector immutability\n\n\"Once a DaemonSet is created, its `.spec.selector` can not be mutated,\" \"precisely because\nmutating a pod selector can lead to the unintentional orphaning of Pods\"\n[source: k8s-docs-daemonset-2026-08-24]. **The only workload resource in the chapter that\nforbids the edit outright**, and Chapter 6 uses it as evidence for how hazardous selector\nmutation is in general. See `owner-reference.md`.\n\n## ✅ The Chapter 7 handoff — verified, and Stage 13 got it backwards\n\n`ch-06:894` plants the tease and points correctly at **Ch 7 §4**:\n\n> DaemonSets keep running on nodes where **nothing else will** — nodes the cluster has fenced\n> off from ordinary workloads entirely. And you have already met the mechanism that makes\n> this possible, **in disguise**: it has been holding those networking and logging agents in\n> place on every node since you first saw them running everywhere in Chapter 3's census.\n> Chapter 7 unmasks it.\n\n**Chapter 7's line 696 callback is accurate as written.** Stage 13 reported both phrases\nabsent from Chapter 6 and recommended rewriting Chapter 7's opening; **that fix must not be\napplied** — it would replace a correct callback with a weaker one and discard the setup\nChapter 7 pays off. This also discharges Chapter 7's ledger debt item #4.\n\n⚑ **A separate pointer at `ch-06:965`, closing §7, says `Ch 7 §5`. That one is wrong** —\nCh 7 §4 is *When the Berth Refuses You* (taints); §5 is *Placing Pods Relative to Each\nOther*. Chapter 6 currently points at two different Chapter 7 sections for the same material.\nOne-token fix.\n\n## Related\n\n[[workload-resource]] · [[taint]] · [[toleration]] · [[nodeselector]] · [[node-affinity]] ·\n[[selector-template-agreement]] · [[owner-reference]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/job.md ===\n# Concept: Job — work that ends\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §7 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Closes:** Chapter 3's reservation (name-dropped in the §2 controller list).\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> Jobs represent **one-off tasks that run to completion and then stop.** A Job creates one or\n> more Pods and continues to retry execution until a specified number of them successfully\n> terminate; as Pods complete, the Job tracks the successful completions, and when the\n> specified number is reached the Job is complete. Deleting a Job cleans up the Pods it\n> created. [source: k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24]\n\n## ★ Fixed Point\n\n> **Job: runs a task to completion, once.**\n\n## It is still the control loop — with a different desired state\n\nThe simple case is \"one Job object reliably running one Pod to completion,\" and the Job \"will\nstart a new Pod if the first one fails or is deleted, for example due to node hardware\nfailure or a node reboot\" [source: k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24].\n\n> **The desired state is *completion* rather than *a count of running things*.** Nothing was\n> added to the loop to accommodate it.\n\nThis is the third of the six desired-states `control-loop.md` is instanced with in this\nchapter, and it is the one that most clearly shows the loop is shape-agnostic.\n\n## Chapter 5's other two Pod phases, finally used\n\nChapter 5 taught five phases and gave immediate use to three. Here are the others:\n`Succeeded` — \"all containers in the Pod terminated in success and will not be restarted\";\n`Failed` — \"all containers terminated and at least one terminated in failure\"\n[source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23].\n\n> For everything in §1 through §6, those two phases were terminal states you hoped never to\n> see. **For a Job, `Succeeded` is the point.**\n\nPractice Q16 grades it, and its distractor C (`Completed`) is a phase that does not exist —\n⚑ correctly softened after Stage 6 F-5 to \"a word you will meet in tooling output,\" because\nno cached source documents the `kubectl get pods` STATUS column derivation.\n\n## The restriction that writes itself\n\nA Job's Pod template \"may only use a `restartPolicy` of `Never` or `OnFailure`\"\n[source: k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24]. `Always` is not permitted.\n\n> Once you have the concept, that restriction writes itself: **a Pod that is always restarted\n> can never complete.**\n\nRetrieves Chapter 5's `restartPolicy` and inverts the meaning of *exited* — which Soundings\nQ6 pre-tests with the web-server-vs-backup-script pair.\n\n## Deliberately out of scope — do not add\n\n`parallelism`, `backoffLimit`, Indexed Jobs, job-history limits and TTL all return **zero**\nhits in the chapter, and the curriculum audit confirmed the boundary held. ⚑ Note also that\n`k8s-docs-job-2026-08-24` carries a **known snapshot error** — it reports\n`.spec.backoffLimit` as 4 where the raw source says 6. The chapter never mentions the field,\nso there is no exposure. **Do not introduce it from this snapshot.**\n\n## Related\n\n[[cronjob]] · [[workload-resource]] · [[pod-phase]] · [[restart-policy]] · [[control-loop]]\n=== END WRITE ===\n\n=== WRITE C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/concepts/cronjob.md ===\n# Concept: CronJob — work that ends, repeatedly\n\n**Home:** Chapter 6 §7 · **Competency:** D1.1 · **Status:** canonical\n**Split from** `job.md` deliberately — see below.\n\n## Definition (verbatim)\n\n> A CronJob **creates Jobs on a repeating schedule**, and one CronJob object is like one line\n> of a crontab file on a Unix system. [source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24]\n\nMeant for \"regular scheduled actions such as backups and report generation.\"\n\n## ★ Fixed Point\n\n> **CronJob: creates the same Job repeatedly, on a schedule.**\n\n## ⚠ Why this is a separate shard — B1 trap #23, `[source]`-backed\n\n> \"Job and CronJob both run scheduled work.\" **No.** A Job runs a task to completion once.\n> A CronJob creates Jobs on a repeating schedule. **The CronJob is not a kind of Job. It is\n> a thing that *makes* Jobs, the way a Deployment is not a kind of ReplicaSet but a thing\n> that makes ReplicaSets. Once you notice that both pairs have the same shape, the\n> distinction stops being something to memorize.**\n\nThat parallel is the reason this shard is split from `job.md`. Deployment and ReplicaSet are\nmodelled as two shards; modelling CronJob and Job as one would break the symmetry the reader\nis being taught to notice. Bearings #3 Q3's key sends a wrong answer back to exactly this\ncomparison.\n\n## The fields\n\n- **`.spec.schedule`** — required, standard Cron syntax, five fields minute through\n  day-of-week. `0 3 * * 1` is weekly on a Monday at 3 a.m.; `0 2 * * *` is daily at 02:00.\n- **`.spec.timeZone`** — a valid time zone name controlling how the schedule is interpreted.\n- **`.spec.jobTemplate`** — \"exactly the same schema as a Job, nested, without its own\n  `apiVersion` or `kind`.\"\n\n[source: k8s-docs-cronjob-2026-08-24]\n\n⚑ The nesting move is the same one `pod-template` uses in §1, one level further up. Worth\nnaming as a *pattern* rather than two facts.\n\n## ⚑ The caution that bites in production, and is untested\n\n> A CronJob creates a Job **approximately** once per execution time of its schedule. The\n> scheduling is approximate because there are circumstances where **two Jobs might be\n> created, or none.** Kubernetes tries to avoid those situations but does not completely\n> prevent them, therefore the Jobs you define **should be id"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":618,"cache_read_input_tokens":264746,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":618},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2c44320-c026-4b9a-b495-33fa90758087","uuid":"905670c6-fc42-403d-8f83-9a4d3374e402","timestamp":"2026-08-24T18:59:05.982Z","request_id":"req_011CeN57xDTYoAqTYmhn8h8A"}
